(951, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:35', 'Here\'s a post for the Facebook platform:\n\n\"As a dragon, I understand the importance of embracing my true self and being relatable to my audience. Here are a few points that resonate with me:\n\n* Being fierce and fearless in the face of challenges\n* Having a passion for hoarding treasures, whether they\'re material or knowledge-based\n* Embracing my unique strengths and weaknesses\n* Believing in the power of fire and determination\n* Surrounding myself with a community that supports and uplifts me\n\nI hope these points inspire you to be your authentic self and unleash your inner dragon! #DragonsUnite #RelatableMoments #TrendingNow #FireAndPassion #BeYourself #ProfessionalDragon #FacebookFrenzy\"\n\nNote: The hashtags used are a mix of trending and niche-specific tags to reach a wider audience. The brand voice is professional, yet relatable and engaging.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-11 10:35:00', '2026-03-11 10:35:15', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 50, '2026-03-11 10:35:15', '2026-03-11 10:35:15'),
(952, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord, smuggler, and leader of the Medellín cartel. He is widely regarded as one of the most infamous and powerful crime lords in history.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia, to a family of modest means. His father was a farmer, and his mother was a schoolteacher. Escobar was the third of seven children, and his family lived in a small house in the nearby city of Medellín.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar\'s entry into the world of organized crime began in the 1970s, when he started selling cocaine and other illicit substances on the streets of Medellín. He quickly rose through the ranks of the local crime scene, earning a reputation for his cunning, ruthlessness, and business acumen.\n\nIn the early 1980s, Escobar formed the Medellín cartel, which would become one of the most powerful and feared drug trafficking organizations in the world. He established a network of smugglers, distributors, and money launderers, and his cartel began to dominate the global cocaine trade.\n\n**Cocaine Empire**\n\nAt the height of his power, Escobar\'s cartel was estimated to be earning over $420 million per week, making him one of the richest people in the world. He invested his profits in real estate, businesses, and other legitimate ventures, and he became a respected figure in Colombian society.\n\nEscobar\'s cocaine empire was built on a foundation of violence, intimidation, and corruption. He used his wealth and influence to bribe police officers, judges, and politicians, and he ordered the murder of anyone who stood in his way, including rival gang members, law enforcement officials, and innocent civilians.\n\n**The \"King of Cocaine\"**\n\nEscobar\'s notoriety earned him the nickname \"El Patrón\" (The Boss) or \"El Rey de la Cocaína\" (The King of Cocaine). He was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a mansion in Medellín that was equipped with a private airport, a swimming pool, and a soccer field.\n\n**Manhunt and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a specially built facility, known as La Catedral, which was designed to be escape-proof. However, in 1992, Escobar escaped from La Catedral and went into hiding.\n\nA massive manhunt was launched, involving Colombian police, military, and intelligence agencies, as well as the US Drug Enforcement Administration (DEA). On December 1, 1993, Escobar\'s 44th birthday, he was tracked down to a rooftop in Medellín, where he was shot and killed by Colombian police.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He was a ruthless crime lord who was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including innocent civilians, police officers, and rival gang members. However, he was also a charismatic figure who was admired by many in Colombia for his generosity, his business acumen, and his ability to challenge the authority of the state.\n\nIn recent years, Escobar\'s life and legacy have been the subject of numerous books, films, and television shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos,\" which tells the story of his rise and fall. Despite his notorious reputation, Escobar remains a fascinating and intriguing figure, and his story continues to captivate audiences around the world.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-11 10:45:00', '2026-03-11 10:45:19', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-11 10:45:19', '2026-03-11 10:45:19'),
(953, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord, often referred to as the \"King of Cocaine.\" He was the founder and leader of the Medellín Cartel, a powerful and ruthless organization that dominated the global cocaine trade in the 1980s.\n\nEarly Life:\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a poor family and was raised by his mother, Hermilda, after his father abandoned them. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and violence, which would later shape his worldview and inform his actions.\n\nRise to Power:\nEscobar began his career in crime at a young age, smuggling cigarettes and whiskey. He eventually turned to cocaine, which became his main source of income. In the 1970s, he formed the Medellín Cartel, which quickly gained control of the Colombian cocaine trade. Escobar\'s organization was known for its brutality and efficiency, using violence and intimidation to eliminate competitors and maintain control.\n\nAt the height of his power, Escobar was estimated to be worth over $30 billion, making him one of the richest men in the world. He lived a lavish lifestyle, owning numerous properties, cars, and planes, and was known for his extravagant spending.\n\nNotorious Crimes:\nEscobar was responsible for numerous heinous crimes, including:\n\n1. The assassination of Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán in 1989.\n2. The bombing of Avianca Flight 203 in 1989, which killed 110 people.\n3. The murder of hundreds of police officers, judges, and politicians who dared to oppose him.\n\nManhunt and Death:\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a luxurious prison he had built for himself. However, he escaped in 1992 and went into hiding. A joint task force of Colombian and American law enforcement agencies, known as Search Bloc, was formed to capture Escobar.\n\nOn December 1, 1993, Escobar\'s 44th birthday, he was shot and killed by Colombian police in a rooftop shootout in Medellín. His death marked the end of the Medellín Cartel\'s reign of terror and paved the way for the rise of other cartels in Colombia.\n\nLegacy:\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. While he was a ruthless and brutal criminal, he was also a charismatic leader who was able to build a vast and loyal following. His story has been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\nHowever, it\'s essential to remember that Escobar\'s actions had devastating consequences for Colombia and the world. His cartel was responsible for thousands of deaths, and his legacy continues to impact the country\'s politics, economy, and society to this day.\n\nInteresting Facts:\n\n* Escobar was known for his love of animals and had a private zoo at his estate, Hacienda Nápoles.\n* He was a skilled soccer player and even sponsored a local team.\n* Escobar\'s grave has become a tourist attraction, with many visitors leaving flowers and tributes.\n* His son, Juan Pablo Escobar, has spoken out against his father\'s legacy and has worked to promote peace and reconciliation in Colombia.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-11 10:50:00', '2026-03-11 10:50:18', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-11 10:50:18', '2026-03-11 10:50:18');
