(1030, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:35', 'Here\'s a post for the Dragon business/creator on Facebook:\n\n**Embracing Your Inner Strength**\n\nAs a dragon, I\'ve learned a thing or two about resilience and perseverance. Here are a few key takeaways that can help you tap into your own inner strength:\n\n* Believe in yourself and your abilities\n* Don\'t be afraid to take risks and face challenges head-on\n* Surround yourself with people who support and encourage you\n* Stay focused on your goals and keep moving forward, even when the going gets tough\n* Remember that it\'s okay to make mistakes - they\'re an opportunity to learn and grow\n\nBy embracing your inner strength, you can overcome any obstacle and achieve greatness. So go ahead, unleash your inner dragon and take on the world!\n\n#DragonMode #InnerStrength #Resilience #Motivation #Inspiration #Trending #Facebook #BusinessTips #PersonalGrowth #SelfImprovement #BelieveInYourself', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-13 10:35:00', '2026-03-13 10:35:16', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 50, '2026-03-13 10:35:16', '2026-03-13 10:35:16'),
(1031, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord and the founder of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and notorious cartels in the world. He is widely considered to be one of the most infamous and successful criminals in history.\n\nEarly Life:\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a poor family and was raised by his mother, Hermilda, and his father, Abel. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and violence, and he was involved in petty crime from a young age.\n\nRise to Power:\nEscobar\'s rise to power began in the 1970s, when he started smuggling marijuana and cocaine into the United States. He quickly built a network of smugglers and distributors, and his business grew rapidly. In the early 1980s, Escobar formed the Medellín cartel, which would become one of the most powerful and feared cartels in the world.\n\nThe Medellín Cartel:\nThe Medellín cartel was a vast and complex organization that controlled much of the global cocaine trade. Escobar and his associates used violence, intimidation, and bribery to maintain their power and protect their interests. They also invested heavily in legitimate businesses, such as real estate and finance, to launder their profits and expand their empire.\n\nAt the height of his power, Escobar was estimated to be worth over $30 billion, making him one of the richest men in the world. He was known for his flamboyant lifestyle, which included owning a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a vast estate in Medellín.\n\nDownfall:\nEscobar\'s downfall began in the late 1980s, when the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a crackdown on the Medellín cartel. In 1991, Escobar surrendered to the authorities and was imprisoned, but he continued to run his empire from behind bars.\n\nIn 1992, Escobar escaped from prison and went on the run, sparking a massive manhunt. He was eventually tracked down and killed on December 1, 1993, in a shootout with Colombian police in Medellín.\n\nLegacy:\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning criminal who was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including politicians, police officers, and innocent civilians. However, he is also remembered as a charismatic and calculating leader who was able to build a vast and powerful empire through his intelligence, charm, and strategic thinking.\n\nIn recent years, Escobar\'s life and legacy have been the subject of numerous books, films, and television shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\" His story continues to fascinate and horrify people around the world, and his name has become synonymous with the darker side of human nature.\n\nSome interesting facts about Pablo Escobar:\n\n* Escobar was known for his love of soccer and was a major sponsor of the Atlético Nacional soccer team in Medellín.\n* He was a skilled pilot and owned several aircraft, including a Learjet and a helicopter.\n* Escobar was a prolific builder and constructed many houses, apartments, and other buildings in Medellín, including the famous Hacienda Nápoles, his private estate.\n* He was a fan of the American gangster movie \"The Godfather\" and saw himself as a kind of Colombian Don Corleone.\n* Escobar\'s favorite car was the Mercedes-Benz 600, and he owned several of them.\n* He was known for his generosity to the poor and was often referred to as \"El Patrón\" (The Boss) by the people of Medellín.\n\nOverall, Pablo Escobar was a complex and fascinating figure who left an indelible mark on the world. His story is a cautionary tale about the dangers of greed, ambition, and the corrupting influence of power.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-13 10:45:00', '2026-03-13 10:45:19', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-13 10:45:19', '2026-03-13 10:45:19'),
(1032, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord, smuggler, and leader of the Medellín Cartel. He is widely regarded as one of the most infamous and powerful criminals of the 20th century.\n\n**Early Life and Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar was born in Rionegro, Colombia, to a poor farming family. He dropped out of school in the seventh grade and began his life of crime at a young age, selling stolen cars and motorcycles. In the 1970s, he became involved in the cocaine trade, which was rapidly expanding in Colombia.\n\nEscobar\'s rise to power was swift and ruthless. He formed alliances with other traffickers, eliminated his competitors, and expanded his operations to include cocaine production, transportation, and distribution. He also invested in real estate, agriculture, and other legitimate businesses to launder his profits.\n\n**The Medellín Cartel**\n\nBy the early 1980s, Escobar had become the leader of the Medellín Cartel, which dominated the global cocaine trade. The cartel\'s operations were highly organized, with a complex network of producers, smugglers, and distributors. Escobar\'s organization was known for its brutality, using violence and intimidation to maintain control and eliminate rivals.\n\n**Notorious Crimes and Feats**\n\nEscobar was responsible for numerous notorious crimes, including:\n\n1. **The assassination of Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán**: In 1989, Escobar ordered the assassination of Galán, who had vowed to crack down on the cartel.\n2. **The bombing of Avianca Flight 203**: In 1989, Escobar\'s cartel bombed a commercial airliner, killing 110 people, in an attempt to kill a potential witness against him.\n3. **The murder of Colombian police and judiciary officials**: Escobar\'s organization killed hundreds of police officers, judges, and prosecutors who were pursuing him.\n\nDespite his notoriety, Escobar was also known for his charitable works, particularly in his hometown of Medellín. He built housing, roads, and other infrastructure, earning him a degree of popularity among some locals.\n\n**Pursuit and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a specially built facility, known as the \"Cathedral.\" However, he continued to run his cartel from behind bars and eventually escaped in 1992.\n\nA massive manhunt ensued, involving Colombian and American authorities. On December 1, 1993, Escobar was killed in a shootout with Colombian police in Medellín. He was 44 years old.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning criminal who brought immense wealth and power to himself and his organization. However, his actions also led to the deaths of thousands of people, including innocent civilians, police officers, and rival traffickers.\n\nIn recent years, Escobar\'s life has been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV series, including the popular Netflix show \"Narcos.\" His story continues to fascinate and horrify people around the world, serving as a reminder of the dangers of organized crime and the importance of law enforcement efforts to combat it.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-13 10:50:00', '2026-03-13 10:50:19', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-13 10:50:19', '2026-03-13 10:50:19'),
(1033, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:53', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord, crime boss, and one of the most notorious figures in the history of organized crime. He was the founder and leader of the Medellín cartel, which became one of the most powerful and feared drug trafficking organizations in the world.\n\n**Early Life and Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar was born in Rionegro, Colombia, and grew up in a poor family. He began his career in crime as a teenager, smuggling goods and stealing cars. In the 1970s, he became involved in the cocaine trade, which was rapidly expanding in Colombia.\n\nEscobar\'s rise to power was swift and ruthless. He formed alliances with other traffickers, eliminated his competitors, and expanded his operations into new markets. By the early 1980s, he had become one of the most powerful figures in the Colombian underworld.\n\n**Medellín Cartel and Cocaine Empire**\n\nThe Medellín cartel, led by Escobar, dominated the global cocaine trade in the 1980s. The cartel\'s operations were vast and sophisticated, with a network of smugglers, distributors, and money launderers. Escobar\'s organization was responsible for smuggling tens of thousands of kilograms of cocaine into the United States, Europe, and other countries.\n\nEscobar\'s empire was built on violence, intimidation, and corruption. He used his wealth and influence to bribe officials, politicians, and law enforcement officers, allowing him to operate with relative impunity. His organization was also responsible for numerous assassinations, bombings, and other violent acts.\n\n**Personal Life and Extravagance**\n\nEscobar was known for his extravagant lifestyle and flamboyant personality. He owned numerous properties, including a sprawling estate in Medellín, which he called Hacienda Napoles. He was also a fan of soccer and owned a team, Atlético Nacional.\n\nEscobar\'s personal life was marked by controversy and scandal. He was married to María Victoria Henao, and the couple had two children together. However, Escobar was also known to have numerous mistresses and was rumored to have fathered several children outside of his marriage.\n\n**Downfall and Death**\n\nEscobar\'s reign as the \"King of Cocaine\" came to an end in 1993, when he was killed in a shootout with Colombian police. The operation was carried out by a special task force, known as the Search Bloc, which had been tracking Escobar for months.\n\nEscobar\'s death marked the beginning of the end of the Medellín cartel, and his legacy has been the subject of much debate and controversy. While some have portrayed him as a folk hero or a symbol of resistance against the Colombian government, others have condemned him as a ruthless and brutal criminal.\n\n**Legacy and Cultural Impact**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s story has captivated the world, inspiring numerous books, films, and documentaries. The Netflix series \"Narcos\" (2015-2017) is perhaps the most well-known example, offering a dramatized account of Escobar\'s life and rise to power.\n\nEscobar\'s legacy continues to be felt in Colombia and around the world. His empire may have been dismantled, but the Medellín cartel\'s influence on the global drug trade remains significant. Additionally, Escobar\'s story serves as a cautionary tale about the dangers of organized crime, corruption, and the devastating consequences of the cocaine trade.\n\n**Key Facts**\n\n* Born: December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia\n* Died: December 1, 1993, in Medellín, Colombia\n* Founder and leader of the Medellín cartel\n* Estimated net worth: $30 billion (at the height of his power)\n* Responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including law enforcement officers, politicians, and civilians\n* Imprisoned in 1991, but escaped and remained on the run until his death in 1993.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-13 10:53:00', '2026-03-13 10:53:15', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-13 10:53:15', '2026-03-13 10:53:15'),
