(1061, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord, crime boss, and founder of the Medellín Cartel, one of the most powerful and notorious cartels in the world. He is widely regarded as one of the most infamous and successful criminals in history.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a poor family with his mother, Hermilda, and his father, Abel. Escobar was the third of seven children, and his family lived in a small house in the barrio of La Paz.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar began his career in crime at a young age, starting with small-time robberies and smuggling. He eventually became involved in the cocaine trade, working for various cartels and building a reputation as a ruthless and cunning operator. In the late 1970s, Escobar joined the Medellín Cartel, which was founded by Carlos Lehder and other Colombian traffickers.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín Cartel rapidly expanded its operations, becoming one of the largest and most powerful cocaine cartels in the world. Escobar\'s organization controlled a significant portion of the global cocaine market, and he became known as \"El Patrón\" (The Boss). The cartel\'s success was fueled by Escobar\'s innovative tactics, including the use of small planes to smuggle cocaine into the United States.\n\n**Violence and Terror**\n\nEscobar\'s reign was marked by brutal violence and terror. He was known for his ruthless tactics, including assassinations, bombings, and kidnappings. He targeted not only rival cartels but also law enforcement officials, politicians, and civilians. Escobar\'s organization was responsible for numerous massacres, including the infamous DAS Building bombing in Bogotá, which killed over 50 people.\n\n**Manhunt and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a specially built prison, La Catedral. However, he continued to run his cartel from behind bars, and in 1992, he escaped from the prison. A massive manhunt ensued, with the Colombian government and the US DEA working together to track down Escobar.\n\nOn December 1, 1993, Escobar was shot and killed by Colombian police in a rooftop shootout in Medellín. He was 44 years old at the time of his death.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning crime lord who brought terror and violence to Colombia and beyond. However, he is also celebrated by some as a folk hero and a symbol of resistance against the Colombian government and the US DEA. His life has been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included owning a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a mansion with a private airstrip.\n* He was a skilled entrepreneur and businessman, and his cartel was estimated to be worth over $30 billion at its peak.\n* Escobar was a prolific philanthropist, and he built numerous schools, hospitals, and other infrastructure projects in Medellín and other parts of Colombia.\n* He was a fan of soccer, and he owned a soccer team, Atlético Nacional, which won several championships during his ownership.\n\nOverall, Pablo Escobar\'s life and legacy are a fascinating and complex topic, and his impact on the world of organized crime and beyond continues to be felt today.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-14 10:45:00', '2026-03-14 10:45:17', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-14 10:45:17', '2026-03-14 10:45:17'),
(1062, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord and leader of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and feared organized crime groups in the world. He is widely regarded as one of the most infamous and successful drug traffickers in history.\n\n**Early Life and Rise to Power**\n\nPablo Escobar was born in Rionegro, Colombia, on December 1, 1949. He grew up in a poor family and was raised in the city of Medellín. Escobar became involved in crime at a young age, starting with small-time theft and eventually moving on to more serious offenses, such as kidnapping and murder.\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar began to build his empire in the cocaine trade, which was then a relatively new and lucrative business. He quickly established himself as a major player in the Medellín cartel, using his cunning, ruthlessness, and charisma to eliminate rivals and consolidate power.\n\n**The Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel became one of the most powerful and feared organized crime groups in the world. The cartel\'s main business was cocaine trafficking, and Escobar\'s organization dominated the global market, with estimates suggesting that they controlled up to 80% of the world\'s cocaine supply.\n\nEscobar\'s success was due in part to his innovative and ruthless business tactics. He was known for his ability to bribe and intimidate law enforcement officials, as well as his willingness to use violence to eliminate rivals and protect his business interests.\n\n**The \"King of Cocaine\"**\n\nAt the height of his power, Escobar was known as the \"King of Cocaine.\" He was estimated to be worth over $30 billion, making him one of the richest men in the world. He lived a lavish lifestyle, with a private zoo, a collection of exotic cars, and a sprawling estate in Medellín.\n\n**The War on Drugs and the Hunt for Escobar**\n\nIn the late 1980s and early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the support of the United States, launched a series of campaigns to dismantle the Medellín cartel and bring Escobar to justice. The war on drugs was marked by intense violence, with Escobar\'s organization fighting back against the authorities with bombings, assassinations, and other forms of terrorism.\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a specially built prison, known as La Catedral. However, he continued to run his empire from behind bars, using his network of loyal associates to coordinate his business operations.\n\n**The Final Days and Death**\n\nIn 1992, Escobar escaped from La Catedral and went into hiding. The Colombian authorities, with the help of the US DEA, launched a massive manhunt to track him down.\n\nOn December 1, 1993, Escobar\'s 44th birthday, he was shot and killed by Colombian police in a rooftop shootout in Medellín. His death marked the end of an era in the cocaine trade, and the Medellín cartel was eventually dismantled.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He was a ruthless and cunning businessman who built a vast empire on the back of the cocaine trade. His reign of terror and violence had a devastating impact on Colombia and the world, and his death marked a significant turning point in the war on drugs.\n\nHowever, Escobar\'s story has also been romanticized in popular culture, with films, books, and TV shows portraying him as a charismatic and fascinating figure. His life and death continue to fascinate people around the world, and his legacy remains a topic of debate and discussion.\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his generosity and philanthropy, and he was often referred to as \"El Patrón\" (The Boss) by his loyal followers.\n* He was a fan of soccer and owned a team, Atlético Nacional, which won several championships during his ownership.\n* Escobar\'s favorite car was the Mercedes-Benz 600, and he owned several of them.\n* He was a skilled pilot and owned several planes, including a Learjet.\n* Escobar\'s estate, Hacienda Nápoles, was seized by the Colombian government after his death and is now a theme park and tourist attraction.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-14 10:50:00', '2026-03-14 10:50:17', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-14 10:50:17', '2026-03-14 10:50:17'),
(1063, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:53', 'Pablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord, crime boss, and founder of the Medellín Cartel, one of the most powerful and feared cartels in the world during the 1980s and early 1990s.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Escobar was born in Rionegro, Colombia, on December 1, 1949, to Abel de Jesús Escobar and Hermilda Gaviria. He was the third of seven children. Escobar\'s family was of modest means, and he grew up in a poor neighborhood in Medellín. He dropped out of school in the seventh grade and began working on the streets, selling fruit and other small items.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar\'s entry into the world of organized crime began in the early 1970s, when he started working for a local cocaine smuggler. He quickly rose through the ranks and became a key player in the Medellín Cartel, which was founded by Escobar and his associates. The cartel\'s main business was smuggling cocaine into the United States, and Escobar\'s charisma, intelligence, and ruthlessness helped him become one of the most powerful men in the organization.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín Cartel became one of the most powerful and feared cartels in the world. The cartel\'s operations were highly organized, with a complex network of smugglers, distributors, and money launderers. Escobar\'s organization was responsible for smuggling massive amounts of cocaine into the United States, and his profits were staggering. At the height of his power, Escobar was earning an estimated $420 million per week.\n\n**Violence and Terror**\n\nEscobar\'s rise to power was marked by violence and terror. He was known for his ruthless tactics, including murder, intimidation, and bribery. Escobar\'s organization was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including police officers, judges, and politicians. He also ordered the assassination of several high-profile targets, including Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán in 1989.\n\n**Manhunt and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a luxury prison he had built for himself. However, he escaped in 1992 and went into hiding. A massive manhunt was launched, and Escobar was eventually tracked down by Colombian police and U.S. DEA agents. On December 1, 1993, Escobar was shot and killed in a rooftop shootout in Medellín.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He was a ruthless and violent crime lord who was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people. However, he was also a charismatic and cunning leader who was able to build a massive and powerful organization. Escobar\'s story has been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his love of luxury and extravagance. He owned several mansions, including the famous Hacienda Napoles, which featured a private zoo and a collection of exotic animals.\n* Escobar was a skilled soccer player and was known to have played in local tournaments.\n* Escobar\'s organization was responsible for the construction of several infrastructure projects in Medellín, including roads, bridges, and housing developments.\n* Escobar\'s death was a major blow to the Medellín Cartel, and the organization began to fragment and decline in the years following his death.\n\nOverall, Pablo Escobar was a complex and fascinating figure who played a significant role in the history of organized crime. His legacy continues to be felt today, and his story remains a source of fascination and intrigue for people around the world.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-14 10:53:00', '2026-03-14 10:53:15', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-14 10:53:15', '2026-03-14 10:53:15'),
