(1089, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:35', 'Here\'s a relatable caption for a dragon-themed business on Facebook:\n\n\"As a powerful and mythical creature, I\'ve learned a thing or two about what it takes to succeed. Here are some key takeaways:\n\n* Embrace your fire: Don\'t be afraid to take risks and push yourself outside of your comfort zone.\n* Protect your treasure: Prioritize your goals and don\'t let anyone or anything get in the way of achieving them.\n* Soar to new heights: Set your sights high and never stop striving for greatness.\n* Roar with confidence: Believe in yourself and your abilities, and don\'t let anyone dull your sparkle.\n\n#DragonSquad #MotivationMonday #MythicalMondays #BusinessTips #SuccessMindset #TrendingNow #FacebookForBusiness #EntrepreneurLife #ProfessionalDevelopment\"\n\nThis caption is professional, relatable, and includes trending hashtags to reach a wider audience on Facebook.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-15 10:35:00', '2026-03-15 10:35:17', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 50, '2026-03-15 10:35:17', '2026-03-15 10:35:17'),
(1090, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord, smuggler, and leader of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and infamous cocaine trafficking organizations in history. He is widely regarded as one of the most notorious and successful drug traffickers of all time.\n\n**Early Life and Rise to Power**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia, to a middle-class family. He grew up in a poor neighborhood in Medellín, where he became involved in street crime and small-time smuggling at a young age. In the 1970s, Escobar began working for various cocaine traffickers, eventually building his own network of smugglers and dealers.\n\n**The Medellín Cartel**\n\nIn the late 1970s, Escobar co-founded the Medellín cartel, which quickly became one of the dominant players in the global cocaine trade. The cartel\'s success was based on its ability to smuggle large quantities of cocaine into the United States, using a network of planes, boats, and trucks. Escobar\'s organization also used violence and intimidation to maintain control over the cocaine trade and eliminate competitors.\n\n**Wealth and Notoriety**\n\nAt the height of his power, Escobar was estimated to be worth over $30 billion, making him one of the richest people in the world. He lived a lavish lifestyle, with a large mansion, private zoo, and collection of exotic cars. He also became known for his charismatic personality and his ability to evade law enforcement.\n\n**Violence and Terror**\n\nHowever, Escobar\'s success was also marked by violence and terror. He was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including rival drug traffickers, police officers, and innocent civilians. His organization was also linked to numerous bombings, kidnappings, and assassinations.\n\n**Pursuit and Death**\n\nIn the late 1980s and early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a series of operations to capture or kill Escobar. He was eventually tracked down to a rooftop in Medellín, where he was shot and killed by Colombian police on December 1, 1993, his 44th birthday.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. While he is widely regarded as a ruthless and brutal criminal, he is also remembered as a folk hero by some in Colombia, who see him as a symbol of resistance against the government and the wealthy elite. His life and death have also been the subject of numerous books, films, and television shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his love of soccer and even owned a soccer team, Atlético Nacional.\n* He was a skilled pilot and owned a number of planes, which he used to smuggle cocaine.\n* Escobar was also a prolific builder and constructed many homes, roads, and other infrastructure projects in Medellín.\n* He was known for his generosity to the poor and was often referred to as \"El Patrón\" (The Boss) or \"El Robin Hood\" by his supporters.\n\nOverall, Pablo Escobar\'s life and legacy are a fascinating and complex topic, reflecting both the darker aspects of human nature and the enduring power of charisma and ambition.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-15 10:45:00', '2026-03-15 10:45:13', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-15 10:45:13', '2026-03-15 10:45:13'),
(1091, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord, smuggler, and leader of the Medellín cartel. He is considered one of the most infamous and successful criminals in history.\n\n**Early Life and Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar was born in Rionegro, Colombia, to a family of modest means. He grew up in a poor neighborhood in Medellín and became involved in crime at a young age, starting with small-time theft and smuggling. In the 1970s, he began working for the Medellín cartel, which was founded by Carlos Lehder and other cocaine traffickers.\n\nEscobar quickly rose through the ranks of the cartel, becoming a key player in the organization\'s operations. He was known for his charisma, intelligence, and ruthless tactics, which earned him the respect and fear of his colleagues.\n\n**The Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel became one of the most powerful and profitable drug trafficking organizations in the world. The cartel controlled a significant portion of the global cocaine trade, with Escobar overseeing the production, transportation, and distribution of the drug.\n\nThe cartel\'s operations were highly organized and sophisticated, with Escobar using advanced technology, including encrypted communication systems and satellite phones, to coordinate his activities. He also established a network of loyal associates and hitmen, known as \"sicarios,\" to carry out his orders and eliminate rivals.\n\n**Violence and Terror**\n\nEscobar\'s reign was marked by extreme violence and terror. He was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including rival drug traffickers, politicians, police officers, and innocent civilians. He ordered the assassination of Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán in 1989 and was also linked to the bombing of Avianca Flight 203, which killed 110 people.\n\n**Manhunt and Death**\n\nIn the early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a massive manhunt for Escobar. The authorities finally tracked him down to a rooftop in Medellín, where he was shot and killed by Colombian police on December 1, 1993.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. While he was a ruthless and brutal criminal, he was also a charismatic and cunning leader who was able to build a vast and powerful organization. His story has been the subject of numerous books, films, and documentaries, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\nDespite his notorious reputation, Escobar remains a fascinating and intriguing figure, with many Colombians still regarding him as a folk hero or a symbol of resistance against the government. However, his impact on Colombian society and the global drug trade has been devastating, and his legacy continues to be felt to this day.\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar\'s net worth was estimated to be around $30 billion at the height of his power.\n* He was known for his love of luxury and extravagance, owning numerous mansions, cars, and planes.\n* Escobar was a skilled soccer player and even built a soccer field in his prison cell while serving time in the 1970s.\n* He was married to María Victoria Henao, with whom he had two children, Juan Pablo and Manuela.\n* Escobar\'s brother, Roberto Escobar, was also involved in the Medellín cartel and served as the organization\'s accountant.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-15 10:50:00', '2026-03-15 10:50:14', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-15 10:50:14', '2026-03-15 10:50:14'),
(1092, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:53', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord, smuggler, and leader of the Medellín cartel. He is widely regarded as one of the most infamous and powerful criminals of the 20th century.\n\n**Early Life and Rise to Power**\n\nPablo Escobar was born in Rionegro, Colombia, on December 1, 1949. He grew up in a poor household and was raised by his mother, Hermilda Gaviria, after his father abandoned the family. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and violence, and he quickly became involved in petty crime to support himself and his family.\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar began to build his cocaine empire, initially working as a small-time smuggler and gradually expanding his operations. He formed alliances with other traffickers and built a network of loyal associates, including his brother Roberto and his closest friend, Carlos Lehder.\n\n**The Medellín Cartel**\n\nBy the early 1980s, Escobar had become the leader of the Medellín cartel, which dominated the global cocaine trade. He used his wealth and influence to bribe officials, intimidate rivals, and expand his operations. The cartel\'s profits were staggering, with estimates suggesting that Escobar earned over $20 billion per year.\n\nEscobar\'s success was built on his ruthless tactics, including murder, extortion, and kidnapping. He was known for his brutal treatment of rivals and anyone who crossed him, and his cartel was responsible for thousands of deaths.\n\n**The War on Drugs and the Hunt for Escobar**\n\nIn the late 1980s, the Colombian government, with the support of the United States, launched a crackdown on the Medellín cartel. The war on drugs led to a series of violent confrontations between Escobar\'s forces and the authorities.\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to the Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a specially built facility, known as the Cathedral, which he had designed himself. However, he escaped in 1992 and went into hiding, sparking a massive manhunt.\n\n**Death and Legacy**\n\nOn December 1, 1993, Escobar was killed in a shootout with Colombian police in Medellín. He was 44 years old. His death marked the end of an era in the global cocaine trade, and his legacy continues to fascinate and horrify people around the world.\n\nEscobar\'s story has been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV series, including the hit Netflix show \"Narcos,\" which explores the rise and fall of the Medellín cartel. His life and crimes have also been the subject of intense debate and controversy, with some seeing him as a ruthless villain and others as a folk hero or a symbol of resistance against the Colombian government.\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a vast estate in Medellín.\n* He was a skilled politician and strategist, who used his wealth and influence to build alliances with other traffickers and corrupt officials.\n* Escobar was a prolific killer, responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including police officers, judges, and rival traffickers.\n* He was also a devoted family man, who loved his wife, María Victoria Henao, and his two children, Juan Pablo and Manuela.\n* Escobar\'s legacy continues to shape the global cocaine trade, with many of his former associates and rivals still involved in the business today.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-15 10:53:00', '2026-03-15 10:53:16', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-15 10:53:16', '2026-03-15 10:53:16'),
