(1152, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord and the founder of the Medellín Cartel, one of the most powerful and infamous cartels in history. He is widely regarded as one of the most notorious and successful criminals of the 20th century.\n\n**Early Life and Rise to Power**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a poor family and was raised by his mother, Hermilda, after his father abandoned them. Escobar dropped out of school in the seventh grade and began his life of crime on the streets of Medellín, selling stolen cars and motorcycles.\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar became involved in the cocaine trade, which was then a relatively small and disorganized industry. He quickly rose through the ranks, using his charisma, intelligence, and ruthlessness to build a network of cocaine producers, smugglers, and distributors.\n\n**The Medellín Cartel**\n\nBy the early 1980s, Escobar had established the Medellín Cartel, which would become one of the most powerful and feared cartels in the world. The cartel\'s success was based on its ability to produce and distribute large quantities of high-quality cocaine, as well as its willingness to use violence and intimidation to eliminate competitors and law enforcement.\n\nAt the height of its power, the Medellín Cartel was estimated to be earning over $20 billion per year, making it one of the most profitable criminal organizations in history. Escobar\'s wealth and influence were so great that he was able to bribe and corrupt many government officials, police officers, and judges, allowing him to operate with relative impunity.\n\n**Violence and Terror**\n\nHowever, Escobar\'s success was also marked by extreme violence and terror. He was responsible for the murders of thousands of people, including rival cartel members, police officers, judges, and politicians. He also orchestrated bombings, kidnappings, and other forms of intimidation to maintain his power and control.\n\nOne of the most infamous examples of Escobar\'s violence was the assassination of Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán in 1989. Galán had vowed to crack down on the Medellín Cartel and extradite Escobar to the United States, where he faced charges of cocaine trafficking.\n\n**Downfall and Death**\n\nIn the early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a major crackdown on the Medellín Cartel. Escobar\'s empire began to crumble as his top lieutenants were arrested or killed, and his cocaine shipments were intercepted.\n\nOn July 9, 1992, Escobar escaped from prison, where he had been serving a sentence for murder. He went into hiding, but was eventually tracked down by Colombian police and killed in a shootout on December 1, 1993, his 44th birthday.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. On the one hand, he was a ruthless and brutal criminal who was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people. On the other hand, he was also a charismatic and cunning leader who was able to build a massive and profitable empire through his intelligence and strategic thinking.\n\nEscobar\'s story has been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos,\" which tells the story of his rise and fall. Despite his notorious reputation, Escobar remains a fascinating and intriguing figure, and his legacy continues to captivate people around the world.\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known as \"El Patrón\" (The Boss) or \"El Señor\" (The Lord) by his followers and associates.\n* He was a fan of soccer and owned a team called Atlético Nacional, which won several championships during his ownership.\n* Escobar was a prolific builder and constructed many homes, roads, and other infrastructure projects in Medellín, which earned him popularity among some locals.\n* He was also a prolific collector of exotic animals, including elephants, lions, and tigers, which he kept on his estate, Hacienda Nápoles.\n* Escobar\'s net worth at the height of his power was estimated to be over $30 billion, making him one of the richest people in the world at the time.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-17 10:45:00', '2026-03-17 10:45:18', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-17 10:45:18', '2026-03-17 10:45:18'),
(1153, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord, narco-terrorist, and founder of the Medellín cartel. He is widely considered one of the most notorious and powerful crime lords in history.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nEscobar was born in Rionegro, Colombia, to a poor farming family. He grew up in a tough neighborhood in Medellín, where he became involved in street crime at a young age. He dropped out of school in the seventh grade and began working as a car thief and a smuggler.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar started working for a local cocaine smuggler, Carlos Lehder. He quickly rose through the ranks and became a key player in the Colombian cocaine trade. In 1976, he founded the Medellín cartel, which would become one of the most powerful and feared drug cartels in the world.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel dominated the global cocaine trade, earning an estimated $420 million per week. The cartel\'s operations were ruthless and efficient, with Escobar using violence and intimidation to eliminate competitors and maintain control.\n\n**Notorious Crimes**\n\nEscobar was responsible for numerous notorious crimes, including:\n\n1. The assassination of Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán in 1989.\n2. The bombing of Avianca Flight 203 in 1989, which killed 110 people.\n3. The murder of hundreds of police officers, judges, and politicians who opposed him.\n\n**Pursuit and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a luxurious prison he had built for himself. However, he escaped in 1992 and went into hiding. The Colombian police, aided by the US DEA, launched a massive manhunt for Escobar.\n\nOn December 1, 1993, Escobar was killed in a shootout with Colombian police in Medellín. He was 44 years old at the time of his death.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning crime lord who brought terror and violence to Colombia and beyond. However, he is also seen as a product of Colombia\'s troubled history and a symbol of the country\'s struggles with poverty, inequality, and corruption.\n\nIn recent years, Escobar\'s life and legacy have been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\" Despite his notorious reputation, Escobar remains a fascinating and intriguing figure, and his story continues to captivate audiences around the world.\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n1. Escobar was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a vast estate called Hacienda Napoles.\n2. He was a skilled politician and was elected to the Colombian Congress in 1982.\n3. Escobar was a fan of soccer and owned a soccer team, Atlético Nacional.\n4. He was known for his generosity to the poor and was often referred to as \"El Patrón\" (The Boss) by his loyal followers.\n\nOverall, Pablo Escobar\'s life and legacy are a testament to the enduring power of crime and corruption, as well as the devastating consequences of the war on drugs.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-17 10:50:00', '2026-03-17 10:50:15', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-17 10:50:15', '2026-03-17 10:50:15'),
(1154, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:53', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord, smuggler, and leader of the Medellín cartel. He is widely regarded as one of the most infamous and successful narco-terrorists in history.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia, to a middle-class family. His father was a farmer, and his mother was a schoolteacher. Escobar grew up in a poor neighborhood in Medellín, where he became involved in street crime and smuggling at a young age.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar began working for the Medellín cartel, which was led by Carlos Lehder. He quickly rose through the ranks, becoming a key player in the cartel\'s cocaine smuggling operations. In 1979, Escobar and his partners founded the Medellín cartel, which would become one of the most powerful and feared drug cartels in the world.\n\n**Cocaine Empire**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel dominated the global cocaine trade, smuggling an estimated 80% of the world\'s cocaine into the United States. Escobar\'s organization used innovative methods, such as smuggling cocaine in hollowed-out books, tires, and even submarines. He also invested heavily in bribing officials, politicians, and law enforcement to turn a blind eye to his operations.\n\n**Violence and Terror**\n\nEscobar\'s reign was marked by brutal violence and terror. He ordered the assassination of anyone who stood in his way, including politicians, judges, police officers, and rival cartel members. His organization was responsible for numerous bombings, kidnappings, and massacres, earning him the nickname \"El Patrón\" (The Boss).\n\n**The Hunt for Escobar**\n\nIn the late 1980s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a massive manhunt for Escobar. In 1991, he surrendered to authorities and was imprisoned in a luxurious private prison, which he had built himself. However, he escaped in 1992 and went into hiding.\n\n**Death**\n\nOn December 1, 1993, Escobar\'s 44th birthday, he was shot and killed by Colombian police in a rooftop shootout in Medellín. His death marked the end of an era for the Medellín cartel, and his legend has endured as a symbol of the dark and violent world of narco-trafficking.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. While he was responsible for unimaginable violence and suffering, he also invested in social programs and infrastructure in Medellín, earning him a degree of popularity among some Colombians. His story has been immortalized in films, books, and TV shows, including the hit Netflix series \"Narcos,\" which chronicles his rise and fall.\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar\'s net worth was estimated to be around $30 billion at the height of his power.\n* He was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included owning a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a vast estate called Hacienda Napoles.\n* Escobar was a skilled manipulator and used his charm and charisma to build relationships with politicians, business leaders, and other influential figures.\n* He was a devout Catholic and had a deep respect for the Virgin Mary, often seeking her protection and guidance.\n\nOverall, Pablo Escobar\'s life and legacy serve as a cautionary tale about the dangers of unchecked power, corruption, and violence.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-17 10:53:00', '2026-03-17 10:53:16', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-17 10:53:16', '2026-03-17 10:53:16'),
(1155, 'Auto Post for facebook at 11:18', 'Here\'s a post for the dragon business/creator on Facebook:\n\n\"As a dragon, I understand the importance of embracing our true selves and living life to the fullest. Here are a few things I\'ve learned on my journey:\n\n* Being fierce doesn\'t mean being aggressive, it means being confident and unapologetic\n* Fire can be a powerful tool, but it\'s also important to know when to hold back and show restraint\n* Treasure is not just about accumulating wealth, it\'s about surrounding yourself with things that bring you joy and fulfillment\n* Sometimes, it\'s okay to take a step back and hide in your cave - self-care is important, too!\n\n#DragonsOfFacebook #FireBreathingTips #TreasureHunt #MentalHealthMatters #SelfCareSunday #ConfidenceIsKey #ProfessionalDragons #TrendingNow\"\n\nNote: The hashtags used are a mix of popular and trending ones, as well as some niche-specific ones to reach the target audience. The brand voice is professional, yet relatable and conversational.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-17 11:18:00', '2026-03-17 11:18:16', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 42, '2026-03-17 11:18:16', '2026-03-17 11:18:16'),
(1156, 'Auto Post for facebook at 11:57', 'A pen is a common writing instrument used to apply ink to a surface, such as paper, for the purpose of writing or drawing. It typically consists of a nib or tip that dispenses ink onto the surface, and a handle or barrel that holds the ink reservoir.\n\nThere are several types of pens, including:\n\n1. Ballpoint pens: These use a small metal ball to dispense ink onto the surface.\n2. Rollerball pens: These use a small metal ball to dispense liquid ink onto the surface.\n3. Fountain pens: These use a nib to dispense ink from an internal reservoir.\n4. Gel pens: These use a water-based ink that is suspended in a gel-like substance.\n5. Felt-tip pens: These use a porous tip to absorb and release ink onto the surface.\n\nPens are widely used for various purposes, including:\n\n1. Writing: Pens are commonly used for writing letters, notes, and other documents.\n2. Drawing: Pens are often used by artists and designers to create detailed drawings and illustrations.\n3. Signing: Pens are used to sign documents, such as contracts and agreements.\n4. Journaling: Pens are used by many people to keep a journal or diary.\n\nOverall, pens are a versatile and essential tool that have been used for centuries to communicate and express oneself through writing and drawing.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-17 11:57:00', '2026-03-17 11:58:04', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://pollinations.ai/p/Pen', NULL, 'pending', 16, '2026-03-17 11:58:04', '2026-03-17 11:58:04'),
(1157, 'Auto Post for instagram at 11:59', 'A pen packet, also known as a pen pack or stationery pack, typically refers to a small collection of writing materials, often including:\n\n1. Pens: A set of pens, usually ballpoint pens, in various colors.\n2. Pencils: A few pencils, sometimes including a sharpener.\n3. Eraser: A small eraser for correcting pencil marks.\n4. Notebook or paper: A small notebook or a few sheets of paper for writing or note-taking.\n5. Other stationery items: Depending on the packet, it may also include other items like sticky notes, a ruler, or a bookmark.\n\nPen packets are often used for:\n\n* Everyday writing and note-taking\n* Travel or on-the-go writing\n* School or office use\n* As a promotional item or giveaway\n\nThey can be found in various sizes, from small, compact packets to larger, more comprehensive sets. Some pen packets may also be customized with a company\'s logo or branding.', '[\"instagram\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-17 11:59:00', '2026-03-17 12:00:17', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://pollinations.ai/p/Pen_Packet', NULL, 'pending', 17, '2026-03-17 12:00:17', '2026-03-17 12:00:17'),
