(1189, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:35', 'Here\'s a relatable caption in points for a dragon-themed business on Facebook:\n\n\"As a dragon, I understand the importance of embracing my fiery personality and passions. Here are a few things I\'ve learned along the way:\n\n* Believe in yourself and your abilities - just like I believe in my ability to breathe fire\n* Don\'t be afraid to take risks and try new things - like exploring new caves and discovering hidden treasures\n* Surround yourself with people who support and encourage you - just like my dragon friends do\n* Take time to relax and recharge - whether that\'s taking a nap in a sunny spot or enjoying a quiet evening in my lair\n\n#DragonsOfFacebook #FireBreathingTips #DragonSquad #TrendingNow #BusinessTipsFromADragon #ProfessionalDragon #FacebookFamous #RelatableDragon #DragonInspiration\"\n\nNote: The hashtags used are a mix of trending and niche-specific hashtags to reach a wider audience. The tone of the caption is professional and relatable, while still showcasing the unique personality of a dragon.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-18 10:35:00', '2026-03-18 10:35:17', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 50, '2026-03-18 10:35:17', '2026-03-18 10:35:17'),
(1190, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord and the founder of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and notorious organized crime groups in history. He is widely regarded as one of the most infamous and successful drug traffickers of all time.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nEscobar was born in Rionegro, Colombia, on December 1, 1949. He grew up in a middle-class family and was the third of seven children. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and violence, and he quickly became involved in street crime and gang activity.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar began working for the Medellín cartel, which was founded by Carlos Lehder and other Colombian traffickers. He quickly rose through the ranks, becoming a key player in the organization\'s cocaine trafficking operations. Escobar\'s charisma, intelligence, and ruthlessness made him a natural leader, and he soon became the dominant figure in the cartel.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel became one of the most powerful and feared organizations in the world. The cartel\'s main business was cocaine trafficking, and Escobar\'s network of smugglers, distributors, and money launderers made him a billionaire. At the height of his power, Escobar was estimated to be worth over $30 billion.\n\n**Violence and Intimidation**\n\nEscobar was notorious for his ruthlessness and willingness to use violence to achieve his goals. He ordered the murders of countless people, including politicians, police officers, judges, and rival gang members. Escobar\'s henchmen, known as \"sicarios,\" carried out his orders with brutal efficiency, using tactics such as bombings, kidnappings, and assassinations.\n\n**Manhunt and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a luxury jail that he had built for himself. However, he escaped in 1992 and went into hiding. A massive manhunt was launched, and Escobar was eventually tracked down by Colombian police and U.S. DEA agents. He was killed in a shootout on December 1, 1993, on the rooftop of a safe house in Medellín.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning crime lord, but also as a charismatic and generous figure who invested in his community and supported local causes. Escobar\'s story has been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his love of luxury and extravagance, and he owned a private zoo, a collection of exotic cars, and a sprawling estate called Hacienda Nápoles.\n* Escobar was a skilled politician and formed alliances with Colombian politicians and businessmen to further his interests.\n* Escobar\'s organization was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including innocent civilians, police officers, and rival gang members.\n* Escobar\'s son, Juan Pablo Escobar, has spoken publicly about his father\'s legacy and has written a book about his experiences growing up as the son of a notorious crime lord.\n\nOverall, Pablo Escobar\'s life and legacy are a fascinating and complex topic, and his story continues to captivate audiences around the world.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-18 10:45:00', '2026-03-18 10:45:15', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-18 10:45:15', '2026-03-18 10:45:15'),
(1191, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord, crime boss, and founder of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and notorious drug trafficking organizations in history.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia, to a poor farming family. He grew up in the city of Medellín, where he became involved in street crime and smuggling at a young age. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and violence, which would later shape his worldview and inform his actions as a crime lord.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar began working for various crime organizations in Medellín, including the city\'s notorious \"smuggling gangs.\" He quickly rose through the ranks, earning a reputation for his cunning, ruthlessness, and business acumen. In 1976, Escobar founded the Medellín cartel, which would become one of the largest and most powerful drug trafficking organizations in the world.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel dominated the global cocaine trade, smuggling tons of the drug into the United States, Europe, and other countries. The cartel\'s success was fueled by Escobar\'s innovative tactics, including the use of planes, boats, and other vehicles to transport cocaine, as well as his ability to bribe and intimidate law enforcement officials.\n\n**Violence and Intimidation**\n\nEscobar was known for his brutal tactics, which included murder, kidnapping, and intimidation. He ordered the killing of thousands of people, including rival gang members, police officers, and civilians who stood in his way. Escobar\'s violence was often indiscriminate, and his cartel was responsible for numerous bombings, assassinations, and massacres.\n\n**Personal Life**\n\nDespite his notorious reputation, Escobar was a charismatic figure who was admired by many in Colombia. He was known for his generosity and philanthropy, particularly in his hometown of Medellín, where he built homes, roads, and other infrastructure projects. Escobar was also a devoted family man, and his wife, María Victoria Henao, and their two children, Juan Pablo and Manuela, were his closest confidants.\n\n**Manhunt and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned, but he escaped from prison in 1992. A massive manhunt ensued, with Colombian and American law enforcement agencies working together to track down the fugitive. On December 1, 1993, Escobar was shot and killed by Colombian police in a rooftop shootout in Medellín.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He was a ruthless crime lord who was responsible for countless deaths and suffering, but he was also a charismatic figure who was admired by many in Colombia. His story has been the subject of numerous books, films, and television shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\" Today, Escobar\'s name is synonymous with the excesses and violence of the cocaine trade, but his impact on Colombian society and culture is still felt.\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar\'s net worth was estimated to be around $30 billion at the height of his power.\n* He was known for his love of luxury cars, including a fleet of Mercedes-Benz and BMW vehicles.\n* Escobar was a fan of soccer and owned a team, Atlético Nacional, which won several championships during his ownership.\n* He was a skilled politician and was elected to the Colombian Congress in 1982, but his political career was cut short due to allegations of drug trafficking.\n* Escobar\'s death was met with widespread celebration in Colombia, but his legacy continues to be felt in the country\'s ongoing struggle with crime and violence.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-18 10:50:00', '2026-03-18 10:50:18', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-18 10:50:18', '2026-03-18 10:50:18'),
(1192, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:53', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord, smuggler, and leader of the Medellín Cartel. He is widely regarded as one of the most infamous and successful criminals in history.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia, to Abel de Jesús Escobar and Hermilda Gaviria. He grew up in a middle-class family and was the third of seven children. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and a desire to escape his humble beginnings.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar\'s involvement in organized crime began in the 1970s, when he started smuggling cigarettes and other contraband. He quickly moved on to cocaine, which became his primary source of income. In the early 1980s, Escobar partnered with other Colombian traffickers to form the Medellín Cartel, which would become one of the most powerful and feared drug cartels in the world.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín Cartel dominated the global cocaine trade, smuggling tons of the drug into the United States, Europe, and other countries. The cartel\'s success was due in part to Escobar\'s innovative methods, including the use of small planes and hidden compartments in cars to transport cocaine.\n\n**Violence and Intimidation**\n\nEscobar was known for his ruthless tactics, including murder, intimidation, and bribery. He ordered the killings of thousands of people, including police officers, judges, politicians, and rival traffickers. His cartel was responsible for some of the most notorious crimes in Colombian history, including the assassination of presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán in 1989.\n\n**Personal Life**\n\nDespite his violent and illegal activities, Escobar was a charismatic figure who was admired by some in Colombia for his generosity and philanthropy. He built homes, schools, and roads in Medellín and was known for his love of soccer, which he used to launder money and build his reputation.\n\n**Manhunt and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned, but he continued to run his cartel from behind bars. In 1992, he escaped from prison and went into hiding. A massive manhunt ensued, involving Colombian and American authorities. On December 1, 1993, Escobar was killed in a shootout with Colombian police in Medellín, just one day after his 44th birthday.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. While he was a brutal and ruthless criminal, he also had a significant impact on Colombian society and culture. His story has been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\" Today, Escobar\'s name is synonymous with the dark side of the drug trade, and his story serves as a cautionary tale about the dangers of organized crime and the corrupting influence of power.\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar\'s net worth was estimated to be around $30 billion at the height of his power.\n* He was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a sprawling estate called Hacienda Nápoles.\n* Escobar was a skilled pilot and owned several planes, which he used to smuggle cocaine and evade authorities.\n* He was married to María Victoria Henao, with whom he had two children, Juan Pablo and Manuela.\n* Escobar\'s death was seen as a major victory for law enforcement and marked the beginning of the end of the Medellín Cartel\'s dominance in the global cocaine trade.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-18 10:53:00', '2026-03-18 10:53:17', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-18 10:53:17', '2026-03-18 10:53:17'),
