(1223, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:35', 'Here\'s a relatable caption for a dragon business/creator on Facebook:\n\n\"As a dragon, I understand the importance of breathing fire into your passions. Here are a few things I\'ve learned along the way:\n\n* Believe in yourself, even when others don\'t\n* Take risks and face your fears head-on\n* Surround yourself with people who support and uplift you\n* Never stop learning and growing\n* And most importantly, always stay true to yourself\n\n#DragonSquad #FireBreathingMotivation #TrendingNow #BusinessTips #EntrepreneurLife #MotivationMonday #InspirationalQuotes #SuccessStory #DragonPower\"\n\nNote: The hashtags used are a mix of popular and trending ones, including #TrendingNow to increase visibility. The caption is written in a professional tone, while still being relatable and engaging.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-19 10:35:00', '2026-03-19 10:35:16', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 50, '2026-03-19 10:35:16', '2026-03-19 10:35:16'),
(1224, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord and the founder of the Medellín Cartel, one of the most powerful and feared criminal organizations in history. Here\'s a brief overview of his life and legacy:\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a middle-class family and was the third of seven children. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and a desire to make a name for himself.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar began his career in crime as a teenager, smuggling cigarettes and other goods. He eventually turned to cocaine trafficking, which became his main source of income. In the 1970s, Escobar founded the Medellín Cartel, which quickly grew into a massive organization with tentacles in Colombia, the United States, and other countries.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín Cartel became one of the most powerful and feared organizations in the world. The cartel controlled a significant portion of the global cocaine trade, and its influence extended into politics, law enforcement, and other areas of Colombian society. Escobar\'s organization was known for its brutality, and he was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including politicians, police officers, and rival gang members.\n\n**Notorious Crimes**\n\nSome of Escobar\'s most notorious crimes include:\n\n1. The assassination of Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán in 1989.\n2. The bombing of Avianca Flight 203, which killed 110 people in 1989.\n3. The murder of Colombian police chief Colonel Jaime Ramírez Gómez in 1989.\n\n**Manhunt and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a specially built prison, known as La Catedral. However, he escaped in 1992 and went into hiding. A massive manhunt was launched, and Escobar was eventually tracked down and killed in a shootout with Colombian police on December 1, 1993, in Medellín.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. On one hand, he is remembered as a ruthless and brutal criminal who was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people. On the other hand, he is also seen as a folk hero by some in Colombia, who view him as a symbol of resistance against the government and the wealthy elite.\n\nIn recent years, Escobar\'s life and legacy have been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\" His story continues to fascinate people around the world, and his impact on Colombian society and the global war on drugs remains significant.\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n1. Escobar was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a sprawling estate called Hacienda Napoles.\n2. He was a skilled businessman and investor, and his organization was estimated to be worth over $30 billion at its peak.\n3. Escobar was a supporter of the Colombian soccer team, and he even sponsored a local team, Atlético Nacional.\n4. His brother, Roberto Escobar, was also involved in the Medellín Cartel and was known as \"El Osito\" (The Little Bear).\n\nOverall, Pablo Escobar\'s life and legacy are a testament to the complexities and contradictions of human nature, and his story continues to captivate and fascinate people around the world.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-19 10:45:00', '2026-03-19 10:45:17', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-19 10:45:17', '2026-03-19 10:45:17'),
(1225, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord and the founder of the Medellín Cartel, one of the most powerful and notorious cartels in history. He is widely regarded as one of the most infamous and successful criminals of the 20th century.\n\n**Early Life and Rise to Power**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia, to a middle-class family. He grew up in a poor neighborhood in Medellín, where he became involved in street crime and smuggling at a young age. Escobar\'s early life was marked by violence, poverty, and a strong sense of loyalty to his family and community.\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar began to build his cocaine empire, starting with small-scale smuggling operations. He quickly expanded his business, using his charisma, intelligence, and ruthlessness to recruit and intimidate others. By the early 1980s, Escobar had become one of the most powerful and feared men in Colombia.\n\n**The Medellín Cartel**\n\nThe Medellín Cartel, led by Escobar, dominated the global cocaine trade in the 1980s. The cartel\'s success was built on a combination of factors, including:\n\n1. **Cocaine production**: The cartel controlled vast coca fields in Colombia, Peru, and Bolivia, and was able to produce high-quality cocaine on a massive scale.\n2. **Smuggling**: Escobar\'s organization developed sophisticated smuggling networks, using planes, boats, and trucks to transport cocaine to the United States, Europe, and other parts of the world.\n3. **Violence and intimidation**: The cartel used violence and intimidation to maintain control over its operations, eliminate rivals, and silence law enforcement and politicians.\n\n**Personal Life and Extravagance**\n\nEscobar\'s personal life was marked by extravagance and excess. He built a luxurious mansion, Hacienda Napoles, which featured a private zoo, a lake, and a collection of exotic cars. He also invested in real estate, sports teams, and other businesses.\n\nEscobar\'s family life was complex and often tumultuous. He was married to María Victoria Henao, and the couple had two children, Juan Pablo and Manuela. However, Escobar\'s infidelities and violent outbursts often put a strain on his relationships.\n\n**Downfall and Death**\n\nIn the late 1980s and early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a series of operations to capture or kill Escobar. The cartel\'s violence and intimidation tactics had become increasingly brazen, and the government was under pressure to take action.\n\nOn December 1, 1993, Escobar was shot and killed by Colombian police in a rooftop shootout in Medellín. His death marked the end of an era in the world of organized crime, and the Medellín Cartel\'s power and influence began to decline.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning criminal who brought violence and suffering to countless people. However, he is also seen as a symbol of the failures of the war on drugs and the corrupting influence of power and wealth.\n\nIn recent years, Escobar\'s life and legacy have been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\" His story continues to fascinate and horrify people around the world, serving as a reminder of the dangers of organized crime and the importance of addressing the root causes of poverty and inequality.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-19 10:50:00', '2026-03-19 10:50:13', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-19 10:50:13', '2026-03-19 10:50:13'),
(1226, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:53', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord, smuggler, and leader of the Medellín Cartel. He is widely considered one of the most infamous and successful criminals of the 20th century.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a poor family and was the third of seven children. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and violence, and he quickly turned to crime to support himself and his family.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar began his career in crime as a small-time smuggler and thief, but he quickly rose through the ranks of the Medellín Cartel, which was founded by his cousin, Gustavo Gaviria. In the 1970s and 1980s, Escobar became one of the cartel\'s top leaders, and his success was fueled by his ruthless tactics, strategic thinking, and ability to corrupt and intimidate law enforcement officials.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín Cartel became one of the most powerful and feared organizations in the world. The cartel\'s main business was cocaine trafficking, and Escobar\'s network of smugglers, distributors, and dealers spanned the globe. At the height of his power, Escobar was estimated to be earning over $420 million per week, making him one of the richest people in the world.\n\n**Violence and Intimidation**\n\nEscobar was known for his brutal tactics, which included murder, torture, and intimidation. He was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including law enforcement officials, politicians, and rival gang members. Escobar also used violence to maintain control over his organization and to eliminate anyone who threatened his power.\n\n**Pursuit and Death**\n\nIn the late 1980s and early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a series of operations to capture or kill Escobar. In 1991, Escobar surrendered to the authorities and was imprisoned, but he escaped in 1992. A massive manhunt ensued, and on December 1, 1993, Escobar was killed in a shootout with Colombian police in Medellín.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning criminal who was responsible for immense suffering and violence. However, he is also remembered as a charismatic and calculating leader who was able to build a vast and powerful organization. Escobar\'s story has been the subject of numerous books, films, and television shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included owning a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a sprawling estate called Hacienda Napoles.\n* Escobar was a skilled politician and was able to build alliances with other cartels and organizations.\n* Escobar\'s organization was responsible for the murder of over 4,000 people, including 600 police officers.\n* Escobar\'s net worth at the height of his power was estimated to be over $30 billion.\n\nOverall, Pablo Escobar was a larger-than-life figure who left an indelible mark on the world of organized crime. His story is a fascinating and terrifying look at the darker side of human nature and the consequences of unchecked power and greed.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-19 10:53:00', '2026-03-19 10:53:18', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-19 10:53:18', '2026-03-19 10:53:18'),
