(1312, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord and leader of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and feared organized crime groups in history. Here are some key facts about his life and reign:\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia, to a family of modest means. His father was a farmer, and his mother was a schoolteacher. Escobar grew up in a poor neighborhood in Medellín, where he became involved in crime at a young age.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar started his career in crime as a car thief and a smuggler, but he soon became involved in the cocaine trade. In the 1970s, he partnered with other traffickers, including Carlos Lehder, to form the Medellín cartel. The cartel\'s success was fueled by Escobar\'s ruthless tactics, his ability to evade law enforcement, and the growing demand for cocaine in the United States.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nAt its peak, the Medellín cartel was responsible for supplying up to 80% of the cocaine entering the United States. Escobar\'s organization was known for its brutality, and he was feared by his enemies and respected by his allies. He was also known for his charisma and his ability to build relationships with other powerful figures in Colombia.\n\n**Violence and Terror**\n\nEscobar\'s reign was marked by violence and terror. He was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including police officers, judges, politicians, and rival traffickers. He also ordered the bombing of a commercial airliner, Avianca Flight 203, which killed 110 people. His organization was also responsible for the assassination of Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán in 1989.\n\n**Manhunt and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a luxurious prison he had built for himself. However, he escaped in 1992 and went into hiding. A massive manhunt was launched, and Escobar was eventually tracked down by Colombian police and the US Drug Enforcement Administration (DEA). He was killed in a shootout on December 1, 1993, on the rooftop of a safe house in Medellín.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He was a ruthless and brutal leader who was responsible for immense suffering and violence. However, he was also a charismatic figure who was able to build a vast and powerful organization, and his story has captivated people around the world. His life has been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included owning a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a vast estate called Hacienda Napoles.\n* He was a skilled soccer player and was known to have played in local tournaments.\n* Escobar was a devout Catholic and was known to have made large donations to the Catholic Church.\n* He was also a supporter of the poor and was known to have provided financial assistance to many families in Medellín.\n\nOverall, Pablo Escobar\'s life and reign were marked by violence, power, and extravagance. His legacy continues to fascinate and repel people around the world, and his story serves as a cautionary tale about the dangers of organized crime and the corrupting influence of power.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-22 10:45:00', '2026-03-22 10:45:14', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-22 10:45:14', '2026-03-22 10:45:14'),
(1313, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord, crime boss, and founder of the Medellín Cartel. He is widely considered one of the most notorious and influential figures in the history of organized crime.\n\n**Early Life and Rise to Power**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born in Rionegro, Colombia, on December 1, 1949. He grew up in a poor family and was raised by his mother, Hermilda, after his father abandoned the family. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and violence, which would later shape his worldview and inform his actions.\n\nEscobar began his involvement in organized crime at a young age, initially working as a small-time smuggler and thief. He quickly rose through the ranks of the Medellín Cartel, which was founded by Carlos Lehder and other Colombian traffickers. Escobar\'s charisma, intelligence, and ruthlessness earned him the respect and loyalty of his peers, and he soon became a key player in the cartel.\n\n**The Medellín Cartel and Cocaine Empire**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín Cartel became one of the most powerful and feared organized crime groups in the world. The cartel\'s primary business was the production and distribution of cocaine, which was smuggled into the United States and other countries. Escobar\'s organization was known for its brutality, using violence and intimidation to maintain control over the cocaine trade.\n\nAt the height of his power, Escobar was estimated to be worth over $30 billion, making him one of the richest men in the world. He lived a lavish lifestyle, owning numerous homes, cars, and other luxury items. He also invested heavily in his community, building roads, schools, and hospitals, which earned him a degree of popularity and loyalty among some Colombians.\n\n**Violence and Terror**\n\nHowever, Escobar\'s success was built on a foundation of violence and terror. He was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including rival gang members, police officers, judges, and politicians. His organization was also involved in bombings, kidnappings, and other forms of terrorism.\n\nOne of the most notorious examples of Escobar\'s violence was the assassination of Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán in 1989. Galán was a vocal opponent of Escobar and the Medellín Cartel, and his murder was widely seen as a warning to others who dared to challenge the cartel\'s power.\n\n**Downfall and Death**\n\nEscobar\'s reign of terror eventually came to an end in 1993, when he was killed in a shootout with Colombian police. The police had been tracking Escobar for months, and they finally cornered him in a rooftop hideout in Medellín. Escobar was shot and killed, along with his bodyguard, Álvaro de Jesús Agudelo.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. On the one hand, he was a ruthless and brutal criminal who was responsible for countless deaths and suffering. On the other hand, he was a charismatic and cunning leader who was able to build a vast and powerful organization.\n\nIn recent years, Escobar\'s life and legacy have been the subject of numerous films, books, and documentaries, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\" While some have criticized these portrayals for glamorizing or romanticizing Escobar\'s life, they have also helped to raise awareness about the devastating impact of the cocaine trade and the importance of addressing the root causes of organized crime.\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his love of animals, and he kept a private zoo at his estate, Hacienda Napoles.\n* He was a skilled soccer player and even sponsored a local soccer team in Medellín.\n* Escobar was a prolific builder and invested heavily in infrastructure projects, including roads, bridges, and hospitals.\n* He was known for his generosity and philanthropy, and he often gave money and gifts to poor families and charities.\n* Escobar\'s brother, Roberto Escobar, is a former accountant and has written a book about his brother\'s life and legacy.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-22 10:50:00', '2026-03-22 10:50:16', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-22 10:50:16', '2026-03-22 10:50:16'),
(1314, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:53', 'Pablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord, crime boss, and founder of the Medellín cartel. He is widely considered one of the most notorious and influential criminals of the 20th century.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Escobar was born in Rionegro, Colombia, to a farmer and a schoolteacher. He grew up in a middle-class family and was the third of seven children. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and a desire to escape his humble beginnings. He dropped out of school in the seventh grade and began working as a car thief and a smuggler.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar began to build his crime empire by smuggling cocaine from Colombia to the United States. He quickly became one of the most prominent figures in the Colombian cocaine trade, earning the nickname \"El Patrón\" (The Boss). Escobar\'s success was fueled by his cunning, ruthlessness, and ability to corrupt government officials and law enforcement.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nIn the early 1980s, Escobar founded the Medellín cartel, which became one of the largest and most powerful cocaine-trafficking organizations in the world. The cartel\'s operations were highly organized, with a network of smugglers, distributors, and money launderers. Escobar\'s organization was responsible for smuggling hundreds of tons of cocaine into the United States each year, earning him an estimated $30 billion in revenue.\n\n**Violence and Intimidation**\n\nEscobar\'s reign was marked by violence and intimidation. He ordered the killings of thousands of people, including politicians, judges, police officers, and rival gang members. Escobar\'s organization was known for its brutal tactics, including bombings, kidnappings, and assassinations. He was also responsible for the deaths of several high-profile targets, including Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán and newspaper editor Guillermo Cano.\n\n**Manhunt and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a luxurious private prison, which he had built himself. However, he escaped from prison in 1992 and went into hiding. A massive manhunt was launched, involving the Colombian police, the U.S. DEA, and other law enforcement agencies. On December 1, 1993, Escobar was killed in a shootout with Colombian police in Medellín. He was 44 years old at the time of his death.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning crime lord who was responsible for thousands of deaths and the destruction of countless lives. However, he is also seen as a folk hero by some in Colombia, who view him as a symbol of resistance against the government and the wealthy elite. Escobar\'s story has been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included owning a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a mansion with a private airport.\n* He was a skilled politician and was able to manipulate the Colombian government and media to his advantage.\n* Escobar\'s organization was responsible for the bombing of Avianca Flight 203, which killed 110 people.\n* He was a prolific philanthropist, donating millions of dollars to charitable causes and building homes and infrastructure for the poor in Medellín.\n* Escobar\'s death was met with widespread celebration in Colombia, with many people taking to the streets to rejoice at the news of his demise.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-22 10:53:00', '2026-03-22 10:53:15', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-22 10:53:15', '2026-03-22 10:53:15'),
