(1340, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:35', 'Here\'s a relatable caption in points for a dragon-themed business on Facebook:\n\n\"As a dragon, I understand the importance of embracing my true nature and living my best life. Here are a few things I\'ve learned along the way:\n\n* Believe in yourself and your abilities, just like I believe in my fire-breathing skills\n* Don\'t be afraid to take risks and try new things, like exploring new territories or trying new foods\n* Surround yourself with people who support and encourage you, just like my dragon friends do for me\n* Take time to relax and recharge, whether that\'s by taking a nap in a sunny spot or enjoying a good book\n\n#DragonLife #FireBreathing #SelfCare #MentalHealthMatters #BelieveInYourself #NewBeginnings #DragonSquad #TrendingNow #FacebookFamous #DragonLove\"\n\nNote: The hashtags used are a mix of popular and trending ones, as well as some specific to the dragon theme. Feel free to adjust or add more based on your specific needs and audience.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-23 10:35:00', '2026-03-23 10:35:14', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 50, '2026-03-23 10:35:14', '2026-03-23 10:35:14'),
(1341, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord and the leader of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and notorious criminal organizations in history. He is widely regarded as one of the most infamous and successful drug traffickers of all time.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia, to a family of modest means. His father was a farmer, and his mother was a schoolteacher. Escobar grew up in a poor neighborhood in Medellín, where he became involved in street crime at a young age. He dropped out of school in the seventh grade and began working as a car thief and a smuggler.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar became involved in the cocaine trade, which was then a relatively new and lucrative business. He quickly rose through the ranks of the Medellín cartel, which was founded by Carlos Lehder and other Colombian traffickers. Escobar\'s charisma, intelligence, and ruthlessness made him a natural leader, and he soon became the dominant figure in the cartel.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel became one of the most powerful and feared organizations in the world. The cartel controlled a significant portion of the global cocaine trade, and Escobar\'s network of smugglers, distributors, and money launderers spanned the globe. At the height of his power, Escobar was estimated to be worth over $30 billion, making him one of the richest men in the world.\n\n**Violence and Intimidation**\n\nEscobar was known for his brutal tactics, which included murder, intimidation, and bribery. He was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including police officers, judges, politicians, and rival gang members. He also used violence and intimidation to maintain control over his organization and to eliminate anyone who stood in his way.\n\n**Pursuit and Death**\n\nIn the late 1980s and early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the support of the United States, launched a crackdown on the Medellín cartel. Escobar was pursued by a special task force, known as the Search Bloc, which was led by Colonel Hugo Martínez. On December 1, 1993, Escobar was killed in a shootout with the Search Bloc in Medellín. He was 44 years old at the time of his death.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning criminal who was responsible for immense suffering and violence. However, he is also remembered as a charismatic and cunning leader who was able to build a vast and powerful organization from scratch. His story has been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a sprawling estate in Medellín.\n* He was a skilled soccer player and was known to have played in local tournaments.\n* Escobar was a fan of the Colombian soccer team and was known to have bribed players and officials to influence the outcome of matches.\n* He was a devout Catholic and was known to have made large donations to the Catholic Church.\n* Escobar\'s death was seen as a major victory for the Colombian government and the international community, but it also created a power vacuum that led to further violence and instability in Colombia.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-23 10:45:00', '2026-03-23 10:45:14', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-23 10:45:14', '2026-03-23 10:45:14'),
(1342, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord and the founder of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and feared organized crime groups in the world. He is widely regarded as one of the most notorious and influential criminals of the 20th century.\n\n**Early Life and Rise to Power**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia, to a poor farming family. He grew up in a humble environment and was the third of seven children. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and violence, which would later shape his worldview and inform his actions as a crime lord.\n\nEscobar began his criminal career in the 1970s, initially involved in small-time smuggling and theft. He quickly rose through the ranks, however, and by the early 1980s, he had become a major player in the Colombian cocaine trade. His success was fueled by his cunning, ruthlessness, and ability to build relationships with other powerful crime figures.\n\n**The Medellín Cartel**\n\nEscobar\'s organization, the Medellín cartel, dominated the global cocaine trade in the 1980s and early 1990s. At its peak, the cartel was responsible for smuggling an estimated 80% of the world\'s cocaine, with revenues of over $20 billion per year. The cartel\'s operations were characterized by brutality, intimidation, and violence, with Escobar using terror tactics to maintain control and eliminate rivals.\n\n**Notorious Crimes and Escapades**\n\nEscobar was responsible for numerous high-profile crimes, including:\n\n1. **The assassination of Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán** (1989): Escobar ordered the murder of Galán, who was a vocal critic of the cartel and a potential threat to Escobar\'s power.\n2. **The bombing of Avianca Flight 203** (1989): The cartel planted a bomb on a commercial airliner, killing 110 people, in an attempt to intimidate the Colombian government and eliminate potential witnesses.\n3. **The murder of Colombian police officers and judges**: Escobar targeted law enforcement officials and judges who were investigating his activities or attempting to bring him to justice.\n\n**Pursuit and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a specially built facility, known as La Catedral, which he had designed himself. However, he escaped from the prison in 1992 and went into hiding.\n\nThe Colombian government, with the assistance of the United States, launched a massive manhunt for Escobar. On December 1, 1993, Escobar was shot and killed by Colombian police in a rooftop shootout in Medellín. His death marked the end of the Medellín cartel\'s reign of terror and paved the way for the dismantling of other major Colombian cartels.\n\n**Legacy and Cultural Impact**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s life and legacy have been the subject of numerous books, films, and documentaries, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\" His story has captivated audiences worldwide, with many fascinated by the extremes of his wealth, power, and violence.\n\nHowever, it is essential to remember that Escobar\'s actions were responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including innocent civilians, police officers, and rival gang members. His legacy serves as a reminder of the devastating consequences of organized crime and the importance of combating drug trafficking and violence.\n\nIn recent years, Escobar\'s family has attempted to rebrand his image, with his son, Juan Pablo Escobar, writing a book and creating a documentary series that portrays his father as a complex and multifaceted figure. However, many Colombians and others around the world remain skeptical of these efforts, viewing Escobar as a ruthless and brutal crime lord who brought immense harm to his country and the world.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-23 10:50:00', '2026-03-23 10:50:17', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-23 10:50:17', '2026-03-23 10:50:17'),
(1343, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:53', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord, smuggler, and leader of the Medellín cartel. He is widely regarded as one of the most successful and infamous drug traffickers in history.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a poor household with his mother, Hermilda, and his father, Abel. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and limited opportunities, which may have contributed to his eventual involvement in crime.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar began his career in crime as a teenager, stealing cars and selling stolen goods on the black market. In the 1970s, he became involved in the cocaine trade, which was rapidly expanding in Colombia. Escobar quickly rose through the ranks, becoming a key figure in the Medellín cartel, which was founded by Carlos Lehder and others.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel became one of the most powerful and feared drug cartels in the world. The cartel\'s success was built on a combination of factors, including:\n\n1. **Strategic location**: Medellín was ideally located for cocaine production and smuggling, with access to coca leaf fields in Peru and Bolivia, and proximity to major shipping routes.\n2. **Violence and intimidation**: Escobar and his associates used violence and intimidation to maintain control over the cartel\'s operations and eliminate rivals.\n3. **Corruption**: Escobar bribed and corrupted government officials, police, and military personnel to ensure the cartel\'s operations were not disrupted.\n4. **Innovative smuggling methods**: The cartel developed sophisticated smuggling techniques, including the use of submarines, aircraft, and hidden compartments in ships and vehicles.\n\n**Notorious Acts**\n\nEscobar was responsible for numerous notorious acts, including:\n\n1. **The bombing of Avianca Flight 203**: In 1989, Escobar ordered the bombing of an Avianca airliner, which killed 110 people.\n2. **The assassination of Luis Carlos Galán**: In 1989, Escobar ordered the assassination of presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán, who had vowed to crack down on the cartel.\n3. **The siege of the Colombian Supreme Court**: In 1985, Escobar\'s associates stormed the Colombian Supreme Court, killing several judges and justices.\n\n**Downfall and Death**\n\nIn the early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a major crackdown on the Medellín cartel. Escobar was forced to go into hiding, and his empire began to crumble. On December 1, 1993, Escobar was killed in a shootout with Colombian police in Medellín.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. While he was a ruthless and brutal individual responsible for thousands of deaths, he also:\n\n1. **Created jobs and stimulated local economies**: The Medellín cartel provided employment and economic opportunities for thousands of people in Colombia.\n2. **Built infrastructure and housing**: Escobar invested in infrastructure projects, such as roads, bridges, and housing, which improved living conditions for many Colombians.\n3. **Became a cultural icon**: Escobar\'s larger-than-life personality and notorious exploits have made him a fascinating figure in popular culture, inspiring numerous books, films, and TV shows.\n\nHowever, it is essential to remember that Escobar\'s actions were ultimately destructive and devastating, causing immense harm to individuals, families, and communities. His legacy serves as a reminder of the dangers of organized crime and the importance of promoting justice, equality, and human rights.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-23 10:53:00', '2026-03-23 10:53:19', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-23 10:53:19', '2026-03-23 10:53:19'),
