(1373, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:35', 'Here\'s a relatable caption in points for a dragon-themed business on Facebook:\n\n\"As a dragon, I understand the importance of embracing my fiery personality and sharing it with the world. Here are a few key takeaways:\n\n* **Confidence is key**: Believe in yourself and your abilities, just like I believe in my ability to breathe fire\n* **Passion drives success**: Pursue your passions with enthusiasm and dedication, just like I pursue my love of treasure hoarding\n* **Community matters**: Surround yourself with supportive friends and allies, just like my dragon squad has my back\n* **Continuous learning**: Stay curious and keep learning, just like I\'m always learning new ways to protect my treasure and stay ahead of the game\n\n#DragonMotivation #FireBreathingSuccess #TreasureHuntingTips #ConfidenceIsKey #PassionDriven #CommunityFirst #ContinuousLearning #TrendingNow #DragonSquad #BusinessTips #MotivationMonday #FacebookBusiness #ProfessionalDragons\"\n\nNote: The hashtags used are a mix of trending and niche-specific hashtags to reach a wider audience. The brand voice is professional, yet still relatable and engaging.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-24 10:35:00', '2026-03-24 10:35:21', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://pollinations.ai/p/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds', NULL, 'pending', 50, '2026-03-24 10:35:21', '2026-03-24 10:35:21'),
(1374, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord and leader of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and feared organized crime groups in the world. Here\'s a brief overview of his life and rise to infamy:\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a poor family and was raised by his mother, Hermilda, after his father abandoned them. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and violence, which would later shape his worldview and inform his actions.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar\'s involvement in organized crime began in the 1970s, when he started smuggling marijuana and cocaine into the United States. He quickly rose through the ranks of the Medellín cartel, which was founded by Carlos Lehder and other Colombian traffickers. Escobar\'s cunning, charm, and ruthlessness earned him the respect and fear of his peers, and he soon became the cartel\'s leader.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel became one of the most powerful and profitable organized crime groups in the world. The cartel controlled a significant portion of the global cocaine trade, and Escobar\'s wealth and influence grew exponentially. He invested in real estate, businesses, and politicians, and his network of corrupt officials and informants allowed him to operate with relative impunity.\n\n**Violence and Terror**\n\nEscobar\'s reign was marked by brutal violence and terror. He ordered the murders of countless people, including politicians, judges, police officers, and rival traffickers. His most notorious tactic was the use of \"plata o plomo\" (silver or lead), which meant that those who refused to accept his bribes would be killed. Escobar\'s henchmen also carried out bombings, kidnappings, and assassinations, creating a climate of fear and intimidation in Colombia.\n\n**The Hunt for Escobar**\n\nIn the late 1980s and early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a series of operations to capture or kill Escobar. The hunt was led by the Colombian police and the US Drug Enforcement Administration (DEA), which had declared Escobar a \"most wanted\" fugitive. Escobar went into hiding, using his network of safe houses, tunnels, and loyal associates to evade capture.\n\n**Death**\n\nOn December 1, 1993, Escobar\'s 44th birthday, he was shot and killed by Colombian police in a rooftop shootout in Medellín. The operation was led by the Colombian police and the DEA, and Escobar\'s body was later identified through fingerprints and other forensic evidence.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He was a ruthless and cunning criminal who brought terror and violence to Colombia and beyond. However, he was also a charismatic figure who was admired by some for his cunning and his ability to outsmart his enemies. His story has been told and retold in books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos,\" which chronicles his rise and fall.\n\nToday, Escobar\'s name is still synonymous with organized crime and violence, and his legacy serves as a reminder of the dangers of the drug trade and the importance of law enforcement and justice.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-24 10:45:00', '2026-03-24 10:45:25', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://pollinations.ai/p/pablo_escobar', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-24 10:45:25', '2026-03-24 10:45:25'),
(1375, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord and one of the most notorious figures in the history of organized crime. He was the founder and leader of the Medellín Cartel, which dominated the global cocaine trade in the 1980s.\n\n**Early Life and Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar was born in Rionegro, Colombia, and grew up in a poor but loving family. He dropped out of school in the seventh grade and began his life of crime by stealing cars and selling contraband. In the early 1970s, he started working for a local cocaine smuggler, and by the mid-1970s, he had established his own cocaine trafficking operation.\n\n**The Medellín Cartel**\n\nEscobar\'s organization, the Medellín Cartel, quickly grew into one of the largest and most powerful cocaine cartels in the world. At its peak, the cartel was estimated to be earning $420 million per week, and Escobar\'s personal wealth was estimated to be around $30 billion.\n\nThe Medellín Cartel\'s success was due in part to Escobar\'s innovative and ruthless business tactics. He bribed officials, intimidated competitors, and used violence to maintain control over the market. He also invested in legitimate businesses, such as real estate and banking, to launder his profits.\n\n**Violence and Terror**\n\nEscobar\'s reign was marked by extreme violence and terror. He was responsible for the deaths of hundreds of people, including politicians, judges, police officers, and rival gang members. He also ordered the bombing of a commercial airliner, Avianca Flight 203, which killed 110 people.\n\nIn 1989, Escobar\'s organization was linked to the assassination of Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán, who had vowed to crack down on the cartel. The assassination led to a massive manhunt for Escobar, who went into hiding.\n\n**Capture and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a specially built prison, La Catedral, which he had designed himself. However, he continued to run his organization from behind bars and even had a private phone line and a satellite TV installed in his cell.\n\nIn 1992, Escobar escaped from prison and went into hiding. He was eventually tracked down by Colombian police and killed in a shootout on December 1, 1993, at the age of 44.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He was a ruthless and brutal leader who was responsible for the deaths of hundreds of people. However, he was also a charismatic figure who was admired by many in Colombia for his generosity and his willingness to challenge the status quo.\n\nIn recent years, Escobar\'s life has been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the Netflix series \"Narcos,\" which tells the story of his rise and fall. Despite his notorious reputation, Escobar remains a fascinating and intriguing figure, and his story continues to captivate audiences around the world.\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his love of luxury and excess. He owned a private zoo, a collection of exotic cars, and a mansion with a private movie theater.\n* He was a prolific builder and invested in numerous construction projects, including a soccer stadium and a hospital.\n* Escobar was a fan of soccer and even sponsored a local team, Atlético Nacional.\n* He was known for his cunning and intelligence, and was often referred to as \"El Patrón\" (The Boss) or \"El Señor\" (The Lord).\n* Escobar\'s death was met with widespread celebration in Colombia, but also sparked a wave of violence and instability in the country.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-24 10:50:00', '2026-03-24 10:50:22', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://pollinations.ai/p/pablo_escobar', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-24 10:50:22', '2026-03-24 10:50:22'),
(1376, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:53', 'Pablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord, crime boss, and leader of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and notorious drug trafficking organizations in the world. He is widely regarded as one of the most infamous and successful drug traffickers in history.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nEscobar was born in Rionegro, Colombia, on December 1, 1949, to a poor family. He grew up in a neighborhood in Medellín, where he became involved in crime at a young age. He started out as a car thief and eventually moved on to more serious crimes, such as kidnapping and robbery.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar began to build his cocaine empire. He started by smuggling small quantities of cocaine into the United States, but soon expanded his operations to become one of the largest cocaine traffickers in the world. He used his charisma, intelligence, and ruthlessness to build a vast network of loyal associates and to eliminate his competitors.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nThe Medellín cartel, which Escobar founded and led, was a highly organized and efficient operation. The cartel controlled the entire cocaine production process, from cultivation to distribution, and used advanced technology and sophisticated smuggling techniques to evade law enforcement. At its peak, the cartel was estimated to be earning over $20 billion per year.\n\n**Violence and Intimidation**\n\nEscobar was notorious for his use of violence and intimidation to maintain control and eliminate his enemies. He was responsible for the murders of hundreds of people, including police officers, judges, politicians, and rival gang members. He also used his wealth and influence to bribe and corrupt government officials, which helped him to avoid prosecution for many years.\n\n**Manhunt and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned, but he escaped in 1992. The Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a massive manhunt to capture him. On December 1, 1993, Escobar was killed in a shootout with Colombian police in Medellín. He was 44 years old at the time of his death.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning crime lord who brought violence and terror to Colombia and the world. However, he is also remembered as a charismatic and cunning businessman who built a vast empire and became one of the wealthiest people in the world. His life has been the subject of numerous books, films, and documentaries, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included owning a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a mansion with a private airport.\n* He was a skilled politician and was elected to the Colombian Congress in 1982.\n* Escobar was a devout Catholic and built a church in his hometown of Rionegro.\n* He was known for his generosity to the poor and was often referred to as \"El Robin Hood\" (The Robin Hood) by the people of Medellín.\n* Escobar\'s death was a major blow to the Medellín cartel, and the organization was eventually dismantled by Colombian authorities.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-24 10:53:00', '2026-03-24 10:53:21', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://pollinations.ai/p/pablo_escobar', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-24 10:53:21', '2026-03-24 10:53:21'),
