(1411, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:35', 'Here\'s a possible Facebook post for a dragon-themed business:\n\n**Embracing the Fire Within: Lessons from a Dragon\'s Perspective**\n\nAs a symbol of strength, courage, and resilience, dragons have long fascinated humans. Here are some key takeaways from the dragon\'s world that can be applied to our daily lives:\n\n* **Face your fears**: Just like a dragon confronts its enemies head-on, we too can learn to confront our fears and overcome them.\n* **Protect your territory**: Whether it\'s your personal space or your business, learn to set boundaries and defend what\'s yours.\n* **Breathe fire**: Don\'t be afraid to speak your mind and express yourself passionately.\n* **Soar to new heights**: Aim high and strive for excellence in all that you do.\n\n#DragonSquad #FireWithin #MotivationMonday #LessonsFromMythology #BusinessTips #PersonalGrowth #TrendingNow #InspirationalQuotes #FacebookBusiness #DragonInspiration\n\nNote: The hashtags used are a mix of popular and trending ones, including #MotivationMonday, #BusinessTips, and #InspirationalQuotes, to increase the post\'s visibility and reach a wider audience.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-25 10:35:00', '2026-03-25 10:35:21', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://pollinations.ai/p/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds', NULL, 'pending', 50, '2026-03-25 10:35:21', '2026-03-25 10:35:21'),
(1412, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord, smuggler, and leader of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and notorious crime organizations in history. He is widely regarded as one of the most successful and ruthless drug traffickers of all time.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia, to a poor farming family. He grew up in a tough neighborhood in Medellín, where he was exposed to crime and violence from a young age. Escobar dropped out of school in the seventh grade and began his life of crime, starting with small-time theft and smuggling.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar became involved in the cocaine trade, which was rapidly growing in Colombia. He quickly rose through the ranks of the Medellín cartel, becoming a key player in the organization. Escobar\'s charisma, intelligence, and ruthlessness helped him build a vast network of loyal associates and establish himself as a major player in the global cocaine trade.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel became one of the most powerful and feared crime organizations in the world. The cartel\'s operations spanned the globe, with Escobar\'s network of smugglers, distributors, and money launderers generating billions of dollars in revenue. Escobar\'s success was fueled by his innovative tactics, including the use of small planes to transport cocaine from Colombia to the United States.\n\n**Violence and Terror**\n\nEscobar\'s reign was marked by extreme violence and terror. He was responsible for the deaths of hundreds of people, including politicians, judges, police officers, and rival gang members. Escobar\'s tactics included bombings, assassinations, and kidnappings, which earned him the nickname \"El Patrón\" (The Boss).\n\n**Manhunt and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a luxurious prison he had built for himself. However, he escaped in 1992 and went into hiding. A massive manhunt ensued, with Colombian and American authorities working together to track down Escobar. On December 1, 1993, Escobar was killed in a shootout with Colombian police in Medellín.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. While he was a ruthless and brutal criminal, he was also a charismatic leader who was able to build a vast empire and inspire loyalty in his followers. Escobar\'s story has been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\" Today, Escobar\'s name is synonymous with the dark and violent world of organized crime, and his legacy serves as a cautionary tale about the dangers of unchecked power and greed.\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included owning a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a vast estate.\n* He was a prolific builder and invested heavily in infrastructure projects, including roads, schools, and hospitals, which earned him popularity among some segments of Colombian society.\n* Escobar\'s net worth was estimated to be around $30 billion at the height of his power, making him one of the richest people in the world.\n* He was a skilled politician and was able to manipulate the Colombian political system to his advantage, using bribery, intimidation, and violence to influence elections and policy decisions.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-25 10:45:00', '2026-03-25 10:45:23', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://pollinations.ai/p/pablo_escobar', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-25 10:45:23', '2026-03-25 10:45:23'),
(1413, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord, businessman, and leader of the Medellín cartel. He is widely regarded as one of the most successful and infamous criminals in history.\n\n**Early Life:**\nEscobar was born in Rionegro, Colombia, to a middle-class family. He was the third of seven children, and his father was a farmer. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and a desire to escape his humble circumstances. He dropped out of school in the seventh grade and began working as a street vendor and a car thief.\n\n**Rise to Power:**\nIn the 1970s, Escobar became involved in the cocaine trade, which was rapidly expanding in Colombia. He quickly rose through the ranks of the Medellín cartel, becoming a key player in the organization. Escobar\'s success was due in part to his cunning, ruthlessness, and ability to build relationships with other cartel members and corrupt government officials.\n\n**Medellín Cartel:**\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel became one of the most powerful and feared drug trafficking organizations in the world. The cartel controlled a significant portion of the global cocaine market, and Escobar\'s wealth and influence grew exponentially. He was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included owning a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a sprawling estate called Hacienda Napoles.\n\n**Violence and Terror:**\nEscobar\'s reign was marked by brutal violence and terror. He was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including rival cartel members, police officers, judges, and innocent civilians. Escobar\'s organization was also involved in bombings, kidnappings, and assassinations.\n\n**Pursuit and Death:**\nIn the late 1980s and early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a series of operations to capture or kill Escobar. He was eventually tracked down to a rooftop in Medellín, where he was killed in a shootout with Colombian police on December 1, 1993.\n\n**Legacy:**\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. While he was a ruthless and violent individual, he was also a charismatic leader who was able to build a vast and powerful organization. His story has been the subject of numerous books, films, and television shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts:**\n\n* Escobar was known for his nickname \"El Patrón\" (The Boss).\n* He was estimated to be worth over $30 billion at the height of his power.\n* Escobar\'s organization was responsible for the deaths of over 4,000 people.\n* He was a skilled manipulator and was able to bribe or intimidate many government officials and law enforcement officers.\n* Escobar\'s death was a major blow to the Medellín cartel, and the organization was eventually dismantled.\n\nOverall, Pablo Escobar\'s life and legacy are a testament to the darker aspects of human nature and the destructive power of the drug trade.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-25 10:50:00', '2026-03-25 10:50:24', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://pollinations.ai/p/pablo_escobar', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-25 10:50:24', '2026-03-25 10:50:24');
