(1474, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:35', '**\"Breathing Fire into Your Day: 5 Key Takeaways to Boost Productivity\"**\n\nAs a dragon, I\'ve learned a thing or two about staying motivated and focused. Here are my top tips to help you tackle your day:\n\n* Set clear goals: Establishing a clear plan helps you stay on track and avoid distractions.\n* Prioritize tasks: Focus on the most important tasks first, and then move on to less pressing ones.\n* Take breaks: Taking short breaks can help you recharge and come back to your tasks with renewed energy.\n* Stay organized: Keeping your workspace organized can help you stay focused and avoid wasting time searching for lost documents or supplies.\n* Learn from failures: Don\'t be afraid to try new things and learn from your mistakes - it\'s all part of the growth process.\n\n#ProductivityHacks #MotivationMonday #DragonApproved #BusinessTips #SuccessStrategies #TrendingNow #FacebookBusiness #ProfessionalDevelopment #GoalSetting #TimeManagement #OrganizationTips #LearnFromFailure', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-27 10:35:00', '2026-03-27 10:35:19', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://pollinations.ai/p/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds', NULL, 'pending', 50, '2026-03-27 10:35:19', '2026-03-27 10:35:19'),
(1475, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord, smuggler, and leader of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and feared organized crime groups in the world. He is widely regarded as one of the most notorious and influential figures in the history of organized crime.\n\nEarly Life:\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a poor household with his parents and siblings. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and violence, and he was forced to drop out of school at a young age to support his family.\n\nRise to Power:\nEscobar began his career in crime as a teenager, selling stolen cars and bicycles. He eventually turned to smuggling, and in the 1970s, he became involved in the cocaine trade. Escobar\'s charisma, intelligence, and ruthless tactics quickly made him a leader in the Medellín cartel, which was founded by Carlos Lehder.\n\nMedellín Cartel:\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel became one of the most powerful and feared organized crime groups in the world. The cartel controlled a significant portion of the global cocaine trade, and Escobar\'s wealth and influence grew exponentially. He was known for his lavish lifestyle, which included a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a sprawling estate called Hacienda Nápoles.\n\nViolence and Terror:\nEscobar\'s rise to power was marked by violence and terror. He was responsible for numerous murders, bombings, and kidnappings, and his cartel was known for its brutal tactics. Escobar\'s most notorious act of violence was the bombing of Avianca Flight 203, which killed 110 people in 1989.\n\nManhunt and Death:\nIn the early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a manhunt for Escobar. He was eventually tracked down to a rooftop in Medellín, where he was killed in a shootout with Colombian police on December 1, 1993.\n\nLegacy:\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and brutal leader who was responsible for countless deaths and suffering. However, he is also remembered as a charismatic and cunning figure who was able to build a massive empire and evade capture for so long. Escobar\'s story has been the subject of numerous books, films, and documentaries, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\nInteresting Facts:\n\n* Escobar was known for his love of soccer, and he even built a soccer field at his Hacienda Nápoles estate.\n* He was a prolific builder, and his estate included a private zoo, a lake, and a collection of luxury cars.\n* Escobar was a skilled pilot and owned several aircraft, including a Learjet.\n* He was known for his generosity, and he built several schools, hospitals, and roads in his hometown of Medellín.\n* Escobar\'s net worth was estimated to be around $30 billion at the height of his power, making him one of the richest people in the world.\n\nOverall, Pablo Escobar\'s life and legacy are a fascinating and complex topic, and his story continues to captivate people around the world.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-27 10:45:00', '2026-03-27 10:45:24', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://pollinations.ai/p/pablo_escobar', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-27 10:45:24', '2026-03-27 10:45:24'),
(1476, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord, smuggler, and leader of the Medellín cartel. He is widely regarded as one of the most infamous and successful criminals in history.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a poor family and was raised by his mother, Hermilda, after his father abandoned them. Escobar dropped out of school in the seventh grade and began working as a car thief and smuggler.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar became involved in the cocaine trade, which was rapidly expanding in Colombia. He quickly rose through the ranks of the Medellín cartel, becoming a key player in the organization. Escobar\'s success was due in part to his ruthless tactics, cunning business sense, and ability to build relationships with other cartel leaders.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel became one of the most powerful and feared organizations in the world. The cartel controlled a significant portion of the global cocaine trade, with Escobar estimated to be earning over $420 million per week. The cartel\'s operations were characterized by violence, intimidation, and corruption, with Escobar using his wealth and influence to bribe officials and eliminate rivals.\n\n**Notorious Crimes**\n\nEscobar was responsible for numerous notorious crimes, including:\n\n1. **The assassination of Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán**: In 1989, Escobar ordered the assassination of Galán, who was a leading candidate in the Colombian presidential election.\n2. **The bombing of Avianca Flight 203**: In 1989, Escobar\'s cartel bombed an Avianca flight, killing 110 people, in an attempt to kill a rival cartel leader who was on board.\n3. **The murder of Colombian police officers**: Escobar\'s cartel was responsible for the murder of hundreds of Colombian police officers, who were seen as a threat to the cartel\'s operations.\n\n**Manhunt and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a luxury prison, which he had built himself. However, he escaped from prison in 1992 and went into hiding. A massive manhunt was launched, with the Colombian police and military working with the US DEA to track down Escobar.\n\nOn December 1, 1993, Escobar was killed in a shootout with Colombian police in Medellín. He was 44 years old at the time of his death.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning criminal who was responsible for thousands of deaths and widespread suffering. However, he is also seen as a symbol of Colombian culture and a product of the country\'s troubled history. Escobar\'s life has been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n1. **Escobar\'s wealth**: At the height of his power, Escobar\'s net worth was estimated to be over $30 billion.\n2. **Escobar\'s lifestyle**: Escobar lived a lavish lifestyle, with a collection of luxury cars, planes, and homes.\n3. **Escobar\'s family**: Escobar was married to María Victoria Henao, and they had two children together. His son, Juan Pablo Escobar, has spoken publicly about his father\'s crimes and has worked to distance himself from his father\'s legacy.\n4. **Escobar\'s impact on Colombia**: Escobar\'s activities had a significant impact on Colombia, contributing to the country\'s high levels of violence and corruption. However, his death marked a turning point in the country\'s history, and Colombia has since made significant progress in reducing violence and improving security.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-27 10:50:00', '2026-03-27 10:50:26', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://pollinations.ai/p/pablo_escobar', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-27 10:50:26', '2026-03-27 10:50:26'),
(1477, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:53', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord, smuggler, and leader of the Medellín cartel. He is widely regarded as one of the most infamous and successful criminals of the 20th century.\n\n**Early Life:**\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a poor family and was the third of seven children. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and violence, which would later shape his worldview and actions.\n\n**Rise to Power:**\nEscobar began his career in crime at a young age, starting with small-time hustles and eventually moving into the cocaine trade. In the 1970s, he became a key figure in the Medellín cartel, which was founded by Carlos Lehder and others. Escobar\'s charisma, intelligence, and ruthlessness helped him quickly rise through the ranks, and he eventually became the leader of the cartel.\n\n**Medellín Cartel:**\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel became one of the most powerful and feared organized crime groups in the world. The cartel controlled a significant portion of the global cocaine trade, with Escobar\'s network stretching from Colombia to the United States, Europe, and beyond. At the height of his power, Escobar\'s empire was estimated to be worth over $30 billion.\n\n**Violence and Intimidation:**\nEscobar was known for his brutal tactics, using violence and intimidation to maintain control and eliminate rivals. He was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including police officers, judges, politicians, and civilians. His cartel was also involved in bombings, kidnappings, and other terrorist activities.\n\n**Pursuit and Death:**\nIn the late 1980s and early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a series of operations to capture or kill Escobar. On December 1, 1993, Escobar was shot and killed by Colombian police in a rooftop shootout in Medellín. He was 44 years old at the time of his death.\n\n**Legacy:**\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. While he was a ruthless and brutal criminal, he was also a charismatic figure who was admired by some for his cunning and business acumen. His story has been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts:**\n\n* Escobar was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a sprawling estate called Hacienda Napoles.\n* He was a skilled politician and was elected to the Colombian Congress in 1982.\n* Escobar was a prolific builder and invested heavily in infrastructure projects, including roads, schools, and hospitals.\n* He was a devout Catholic and had a strong sense of family and loyalty to those close to him.\n\nOverall, Pablo Escobar\'s life and legacy serve as a cautionary tale about the dangers of organized crime and the corrupting influence of power and wealth.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-03-27 10:53:00', '2026-03-27 10:53:23', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://pollinations.ai/p/pablo_escobar', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-03-27 10:53:23', '2026-03-27 10:53:23'),
