(1772, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:35', 'Here\'s a relatable caption for a dragon-themed business on Facebook:\n\n\"As a dragon, I know what it\'s like to feel fiery passion and drive. Here are a few things I\'ve learned along the way:\n* Believe in yourself, even when others don\'t\n* Don\'t be afraid to take risks and breathe fire into your dreams\n* Surround yourself with people who support and encourage you\n* Keep learning and growing, just like my scales keep shining\n\n#DragonSquad #MotivationMonday #BusinessTips #EntrepreneurLife #TrendingNow #InspirationalQuotes #DragonFire #PassionDriven #SuccessMindset\"\n\nThis caption uses a professional tone while still being relatable and engaging, and includes a mix of popular and trending hashtags to reach a wider audience on Facebook.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-06 10:35:00', '2026-04-06 10:35:14', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 50, '2026-04-06 10:35:14', '2026-04-06 10:35:14'),
(1773, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord and leader of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and notorious organized crime groups in history. He is often referred to as the \"King of Cocaine\" due to his dominant role in the global cocaine trade.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia, to a family of modest means. His father was a farmer, and his mother was a schoolteacher. Escobar grew up in a poor neighborhood in Medellín, where he became involved in street crime at a young age. He dropped out of school in the seventh grade and began working as a car thief and a smuggler.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar began to build his cocaine empire, initially by smuggling small quantities of the drug into the United States. He quickly expanded his operations, establishing a network of suppliers, smugglers, and distributors. Escobar\'s success was fueled by his ruthless tactics, including violence, intimidation, and bribery.\n\nBy the mid-1980s, Escobar had become one of the wealthiest and most powerful men in Colombia, with an estimated net worth of over $30 billion. He was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a sprawling estate called Hacienda Nápoles.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nThe Medellín cartel, which Escobar led, was responsible for an estimated 80% of the world\'s cocaine production and distribution. The cartel\'s operations were highly organized, with a complex network of smugglers, distributors, and money launderers.\n\nEscobar\'s organization was also known for its brutality, with hundreds of people killed or intimidated to maintain control and silence. The cartel\'s victims included rival gang members, law enforcement officials, journalists, and politicians.\n\n**Downfall and Death**\n\nIn the late 1980s and early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a series of efforts to capture or kill Escobar. The pressure on Escobar intensified in 1991, when the Colombian government, led by President César Gaviria, launched a major crackdown on the Medellín cartel.\n\nOn July 9, 1992, Escobar escaped from prison, where he had been serving a sentence for his crimes. He went into hiding, but was eventually tracked down by Colombian authorities on December 1, 1993. Escobar was killed in a shootout with police in Medellín, on the rooftop of a safe house.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. While he was responsible for immense suffering and violence, he also became a folk hero in some parts of Colombia, where he was seen as a champion of the poor and a symbol of resistance against the government.\n\nIn recent years, Escobar\'s life and legacy have been the subject of numerous books, films, and television shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\" His story has captivated audiences around the world, offering a glimpse into the darker side of human nature and the consequences of unchecked power and greed.\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his love of soccer and even owned a soccer team, Atlético Nacional.\n* He was a prolific builder and constructed over 100 homes for poor families in Medellín.\n* Escobar\'s Hacienda Nápoles estate, which included a private zoo and a collection of exotic animals, was seized by the Colombian government after his death and is now a theme park.\n* The Medellín cartel\'s cocaine production and distribution operations were so vast that they were estimated to have earned over $420 million per week at the height of their power.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-06 10:45:00', '2026-04-06 10:45:17', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-04-06 10:45:17', '2026-04-06 10:45:17'),
(1774, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord, smuggler, and leader of the Medellín cartel. He is widely considered one of the most powerful and infamous criminals of the 20th century.\n\n**Early Life and Rise to Power**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rio Negro, Colombia, to a poor farming family. He grew up in a tumultuous household with his parents and several siblings. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty, violence, and a lack of opportunities.\n\nEscobar\'s involvement in crime began at a young age. He started by selling cigarettes and other contraband on the streets of Medellín, Colombia\'s second-largest city. In the 1970s, he became involved in the cocaine trade, which was then a relatively new and lucrative business.\n\nEscobar\'s rise to power was rapid. He formed alliances with other cocaine traffickers, including the Ochoa brothers, and began to build a vast network of smuggling routes, distribution channels, and money-laundering operations. By the early 1980s, Escobar had become one of the most powerful men in Colombia, with a personal fortune estimated to be over $30 billion.\n\n**Medellín Cartel and Violence**\n\nEscobar\'s Medellín cartel was responsible for smuggling massive quantities of cocaine into the United States, Europe, and other parts of the world. The cartel\'s operations were marked by extreme violence, including assassinations, bombings, and kidnappings.\n\nEscobar\'s tactics were brutal and ruthless. He ordered the killings of politicians, judges, police officers, and anyone else who stood in his way. He also launched a campaign of terror against the Colombian government, which he saw as a threat to his power.\n\n**The Hunt for Escobar**\n\nIn the late 1980s and early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a massive manhunt for Escobar. The operation, known as \"Search Bloc,\" was led by Colonel Hugo Martínez and a team of elite police officers.\n\nThe hunt for Escobar was intense and often brutal. Escobar\'s henchmen and family members were killed or captured, and his properties and assets were seized. Escobar himself went into hiding, using a network of safe houses and loyal associates to evade capture.\n\n**Death and Legacy**\n\nOn December 1, 1993, Escobar\'s 44th birthday, he was shot and killed by Colombian police in a rooftop shootout in Medellín. The killing was seen as a major victory for the Colombian government and a significant blow to the Medellín cartel.\n\nEscobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He was a ruthless and brutal criminal who was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people. At the same time, he was a charismatic and cunning leader who was able to build a vast and powerful organization.\n\nIn recent years, Escobar\'s life and legacy have been the subject of numerous books, films, and television shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\" Despite his notorious reputation, Escobar remains a fascinating and intriguing figure, a symbol of the darker side of human nature and the corrupting influence of power and greed.\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included a collection of luxury cars, private jets, and a zoo with exotic animals.\n* He was a fan of soccer and owned a team called Atlético Nacional, which won several championships during his ownership.\n* Escobar was elected to the Colombian Congress in 1982, but his term was cut short when his criminal activities were exposed.\n* He was known for his generosity to the poor and was often referred to as \"El Patrón\" (The Boss) by his loyal followers.\n* Escobar\'s death was seen as a major blow to the Medellín cartel, which eventually disbanded and was replaced by other drug-trafficking organizations.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-06 10:50:00', '2026-04-06 10:50:16', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-04-06 10:50:16', '2026-04-06 10:50:16'),
(1775, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:53', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord, crime boss, and founder of the Medellín cartel. He is widely regarded as one of the most notorious and powerful criminals of the 20th century.\n\n**Early Life and Rise to Power**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a poor family and was raised by his mother, Hermilda, after his father abandoned the family. Escobar began his life of crime at a young age, selling stolen cars and cigarettes on the streets of Medellín.\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar became involved in the cocaine trade, which was rapidly expanding in Colombia. He partnered with other smugglers and quickly rose to prominence, earning the nickname \"El Patrón\" (The Boss). Escobar\'s success was largely due to his cunning, ruthlessness, and ability to build a vast network of loyal associates and informants.\n\n**The Medellín Cartel**\n\nBy the early 1980s, Escobar had consolidated his power and founded the Medellín cartel, which became one of the largest and most powerful cocaine cartels in the world. The cartel\'s operations spanned from Colombia to the United States, Mexico, and other countries in Central and South America.\n\nEscobar\'s organization was known for its brutality and efficiency. He used violence and intimidation to eliminate rivals, law enforcement, and anyone who stood in his way. His cartel was responsible for numerous murders, kidnappings, and bombings, including the assassination of Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán in 1989.\n\n**Wealth and Notoriety**\n\nAt the height of his power, Escobar was estimated to be worth over $30 billion, making him one of the richest men in the world. He lived a lavish lifestyle, with a private zoo, a collection of exotic cars, and a sprawling estate in Medellín known as Hacienda Nápoles.\n\nEscobar\'s notoriety extended beyond Colombia, and he became a household name in the United States and other countries. He was the subject of numerous books, films, and documentaries, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos,\" which chronicled his life and rise to power.\n\n**Downfall and Death**\n\nIn the early 1990s, Escobar\'s empire began to crumble under pressure from law enforcement and rival cartels. In 1991, he surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned, but he continued to run his empire from behind bars.\n\nIn 1992, Escobar escaped from prison and went into hiding. A massive manhunt was launched, and on December 1, 1993, Escobar was shot and killed by Colombian police in a rooftop shootout in Medellín. He was 44 years old at the time of his death.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. While he was responsible for immense violence and suffering, he also became a folk hero in some parts of Colombia, where he was seen as a symbol of resistance against the government and the wealthy elite.\n\nToday, Escobar\'s name is still synonymous with the cocaine trade and the Medellín cartel. His life and legacy continue to fascinate and horrify people around the world, serving as a reminder of the destructive power of organized crime and the enduring allure of wealth and power.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-06 10:53:00', '2026-04-06 10:53:17', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-04-06 10:53:17', '2026-04-06 10:53:17'),
(1776, 'Auto Post for facebook at 11:18', '**Unleashing the Dragon Within: 5 Key Takeaways for Business Success**\n\nAs a business owner, you\'re likely no stranger to the challenges of scaling your company and staying ahead of the competition. At Dragon, we understand the importance of staying focused, adaptable, and innovative in today\'s fast-paced business landscape. Here are 5 key points to consider:\n\n* **Set clear goals and priorities**: Establishing a clear vision and mission statement is crucial for driving business growth and success.\n* **Foster a culture of innovation**: Encourage experimentation, learning, and innovation within your organization to stay ahead of the curve.\n* **Develop a strong online presence**: In today\'s digital age, having a professional website and social media presence is essential for reaching new customers and building brand awareness.\n* **Build a talented and diverse team**: Surround yourself with skilled and motivated individuals who share your passion and vision for the business.\n* **Stay agile and adaptable**: Be prepared to pivot and adjust your strategy as market conditions and customer needs evolve.\n\n#BusinessSuccess #Entrepreneurship #Innovation #Leadership #DigitalMarketing #TrendingNow #FacebookBusiness #DragonSays #BusinessGrowth #Strategy #Motivation #Inspiration #SuccessTips #BusinessTips #EntrepreneurTips #MarketingTips #LeadershipTips #InnovationTips #TrendingTopics #FacebookTrending #BusinessTrending #EntrepreneurTrending', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-06 11:18:00', '2026-04-06 11:18:15', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 42, '2026-04-06 11:18:15', '2026-04-06 11:18:15');
