(2080, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:35', '**Embracing the Fire Within: Lessons from a Dragon\'s Journey**\n\nAs a dragon, I\'ve learned that success is not just about breathing fire, but about passion, perseverance, and dedication. Here are some key takeaways from my journey:\n\n* **Set goals and chase them**: Don\'t be afraid to aim high and pursue your dreams, just like I do when I\'m soaring through the skies.\n* **Stay focused and adaptable**: The ability to adjust to new situations and challenges is crucial, whether you\'re navigating through treacherous mountains or competitive markets.\n* **Build strong relationships**: Just like a dragon\'s hoard is only as strong as the treasure within, your network and partnerships are vital to your success.\n* **Continuously learn and grow**: Stay curious, keep learning, and always be open to new experiences and knowledge, just like I do when I\'m discovering new lands and treasures.\n\n#DragonInspiration #BusinessLessons #Motivation #SuccessTips #Entrepreneurship #Leadership #PersonalGrowth #TrendingNow #FacebookBusiness #ProfessionalDevelopment #GoalSetting #Focus #Adaptability #RelationshipBuilding #LifelongLearning', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-15 10:35:00', '2026-04-15 10:35:21', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 50, '2026-04-15 10:35:21', '2026-04-15 10:35:21'),
(2081, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord and the founder of the Medellín Cartel, one of the most powerful and notorious drug trafficking organizations in history. He is often considered one of the most successful and infamous criminals of the 20th century.\n\n**Early Life and Rise to Power**\n\nPablo Escobar was born in Rionegro, Colombia, on December 1, 1949. He grew up in a poor family and was raised in the city of Medellín. Escobar began his involvement in crime at a young age, selling stolen cars and other contraband. In the 1970s, he became involved in the cocaine trade, which was rapidly expanding in Colombia.\n\nEscobar\'s rise to power was swift and ruthless. He quickly established himself as a major player in the Medellín Cartel, which was founded by Carlos Lehder and other Colombian traffickers. Escobar\'s charisma, cunning, and brutality helped him to eliminate rival traffickers and consolidate control over the cartel.\n\n**The Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín Cartel became one of the most powerful and feared organizations in the world. The cartel controlled a significant portion of the global cocaine trade, with estimates suggesting that they were responsible for up to 80% of the cocaine entering the United States.\n\nEscobar\'s organization was known for its violence and intimidation tactics. He and his associates used murder, torture, and bribery to maintain control over the cartel and eliminate rivals. The Medellín Cartel was also responsible for numerous bombings, assassinations, and other terrorist attacks in Colombia and abroad.\n\n**Personal Life and Wealth**\n\nPablo Escobar was known for his extravagant lifestyle and his love of luxury. He owned numerous properties, including a lavish estate in Medellín, known as Hacienda Nápoles. The estate featured a private zoo, a lake, and a collection of exotic animals.\n\nEscobar\'s wealth was estimated to be in the billions of dollars, making him one of the richest men in the world at the time. He was known to spend lavishly on his family and associates, and he was particularly fond of buying expensive gifts and luxury items.\n\n**Manhunt and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a specially designed facility, known as the \"Catedral.\" However, he escaped from prison in 1992 and went into hiding.\n\nA massive manhunt was launched by the Colombian and US governments, with the help of the DEA and other law enforcement agencies. On December 1, 1993, Escobar was shot and killed by Colombian police in a rooftop shootout in Medellín. He was 44 years old at the time of his death.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning criminal who was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people. However, he is also seen by some as a folk hero and a symbol of resistance against the Colombian government and the US-backed war on drugs.\n\nIn recent years, Escobar\'s life and legacy have been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV series, including the popular Netflix show \"Narcos.\" His story continues to fascinate and horrify people around the world, serving as a reminder of the dangers and consequences of the drug trade.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-15 10:45:00', '2026-04-15 10:45:27', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-04-15 10:45:27', '2026-04-15 10:45:27'),
(2082, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord and leader of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and feared criminal organizations in the world. Here\'s a brief overview of his life and career:\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a middle-class family and was the third of seven children. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and violence, and he was exposed to the harsh realities of life in the streets of Medellín.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar began his career in crime as a teenager, selling stolen cars and bicycles. He eventually turned to smuggling and trafficking, and his business grew rapidly. In the 1970s, he became involved in the cocaine trade, which would become his main source of income and power.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nEscobar co-founded the Medellín cartel in the late 1970s, along with other prominent Colombian crime lords. The cartel quickly became one of the most powerful and feared organizations in the world, with a stranglehold on the global cocaine trade.\n\n**Violence and Intimidation**\n\nEscobar was known for his ruthless tactics and willingness to use violence to achieve his goals. He ordered the murders of thousands of people, including police officers, judges, politicians, and rival gang members. His organization was responsible for numerous bombings, kidnappings, and assassinations.\n\n**Wealth and Extravagance**\n\nEscobar\'s wealth and influence grew exponentially, and he became one of the richest men in the world. He lived in a lavish mansion, known as Hacienda Nápoles, which featured a private zoo, a lake, and a collection of exotic cars. He also owned a fleet of aircraft and a yacht.\n\n**Pursuit and Death**\n\nIn the late 1980s and early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a series of operations to capture or kill Escobar. He was eventually tracked down and killed in a shootout with Colombian police on December 1, 1993, in Medellín.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning crime lord who brought terror and violence to Colombia and the world. However, he is also seen by some as a folk hero and a symbol of resistance against the government and the wealthy elite.\n\n**Pop Culture**\n\nEscobar\'s life and career have been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\" His story has captivated audiences around the world, and he remains a fascinating and infamous figure in popular culture.\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known as \"El Patrón\" (The Boss) or \"El Señor\" (The Lord) by his associates and admirers.\n* He was a skilled businessman and strategist, and his organization was estimated to be worth over $30 billion at its peak.\n* Escobar was a supporter of the Colombian football team, and he even sponsored a local team, Atlético Nacional.\n* He was a prolific philanthropist, and his organization built roads, schools, and hospitals in poor neighborhoods.\n* Escobar\'s death was seen as a major victory for the Colombian government and the international community, but it also led to a power vacuum that allowed other cartels to rise to power.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-15 10:50:00', '2026-04-15 10:50:17', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-04-15 10:50:17', '2026-04-15 10:50:17');
