(2360, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:35', '**Unlock the Fire Within: 5 Key Takeaways for Aspiring Entrepreneurs**\n\nAs a dragon in the business world, I\'ve learned a thing or two about what it takes to succeed. Here are some key points to consider:\n\n* **Set clear goals**: Define your vision and mission to stay focused on what you want to achieve.\n* **Develop a strong team**: Surround yourself with talented individuals who share your passion and values.\n* **Be adaptable**: Stay agile and responsive to changes in the market and industry.\n* **Invest in innovation**: Stay ahead of the curve by embracing new technologies and trends.\n* **Prioritize self-care**: Take care of your physical and mental well-being to maintain your energy and motivation.\n\n#Entrepreneurship #BusinessTips #MotivationMonday #InnovationNation #SmallBusiness #StartupLife #TrendingNow #FacebookForBusiness #DragonPower', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-23 10:35:00', '2026-04-23 10:35:18', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 50, '2026-04-23 10:35:18', '2026-04-23 10:35:18'),
(2361, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord and the founder of the Medellín Cartel, one of the most powerful and notorious drug trafficking organizations in history. He is widely regarded as one of the most infamous and successful criminals of the 20th century.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia, to a poor farming family. He grew up in a tumultuous household with his mother, Hermilda, and his father, Abel. Escobar was the third of seven children, and his family struggled to make ends meet.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar\'s involvement in crime began at a young age. He started by selling stolen cars and eventually moved on to smuggling cigarettes and whiskey. In the 1970s, he became involved in the cocaine trade, which was rapidly expanding in Colombia. Escobar\'s charisma, intelligence, and ruthlessness propelled him to the top of the Medellín Cartel, which he founded in the late 1970s.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín Cartel became one of the most powerful and feared organizations in the world. The cartel controlled a significant portion of the global cocaine trade, with Escobar\'s network of smugglers, distributors, and money launderers operating in over 30 countries. At its peak, the cartel was generating an estimated $420 million per week in revenue.\n\n**Violence and Intimidation**\n\nEscobar was known for his brutal tactics, which included murder, kidnapping, and intimidation. He ordered the assassinations of politicians, judges, police officers, and rival cartel members. His most notorious act of violence was the bombing of Avianca Flight 203 in 1989, which killed 110 people. Escobar was also responsible for the murder of Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán in 1989.\n\n**Personal Life**\n\nEscobar was married to María Victoria Henao, and they had two children together. He was known for his flamboyant lifestyle, which included owning a private zoo, a collection of exotic cars, and a lavish estate called Hacienda Nápoles. Escobar was also a fan of soccer and owned a team called Atlético Nacional.\n\n**Downfall and Death**\n\nIn the early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a campaign to capture or kill Escobar. In 1991, Escobar surrendered to the authorities and was imprisoned in a specially designed facility. However, he escaped in 1992 and went into hiding.\n\nOn December 1, 1993, Escobar\'s 44th birthday, he was killed in a shootout with Colombian police in Medellín. The operation was carried out by a team of police officers who had been tracking Escobar for months.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning criminal who brought terror and violence to Colombia and beyond. However, he is also seen as a symbol of the failures of the war on drugs and the corrupting influence of power and wealth. Escobar\'s story has been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known as \"El Patrón\" (The Boss) or \"El Señor\" (The Lord) by his associates.\n* He was a skilled money launderer and invested his profits in real estate, businesses, and other legitimate ventures.\n* Escobar was a fan of the Colombian soccer team and even built a soccer field in his prison cell.\n* He was responsible for the construction of over 1,000 homes for poor families in Medellín, which earned him some popularity among the local population.\n* Escobar\'s net worth at the time of his death was estimated to be around $30 billion.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-23 10:45:00', '2026-04-23 10:45:20', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-04-23 10:45:20', '2026-04-23 10:45:20');
