(2525, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:35', '**\"Breathing Fire into Your Business: Lessons from a Dragon\"**\n\nAs a legendary creature, I\'ve learned a thing or two about success and perseverance. Here are some key takeaways:\n\n* **Set ambitious goals**: Don\'t be afraid to dream big and aim high. Whether it\'s conquering a new market or launching a new product, always strive for excellence.\n* **Be adaptable**: The business landscape is constantly changing, and it\'s essential to be flexible and adjust to new circumstances.\n* **Protect your assets**: Whether it\'s your brand, your team, or your intellectual property, make sure to safeguard what\'s important to you.\n* **Innovate and evolve**: Stay ahead of the curve by embracing new technologies and trends.\n* **Build a strong team**: Surround yourself with talented and dedicated individuals who share your vision.\n\n#DragonWisdom #BusinessSuccess #Entrepreneurship #Innovation #Leadership #Trending #FacebookBusiness #ProfessionalTips #MotivationMonday #BusinessLessons #SuccessStories', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-28 10:35:00', '2026-04-28 10:35:20', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 50, '2026-04-28 10:35:20', '2026-04-28 10:35:20'),
(2526, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord, crime boss, and founder of the Medellín cartel. He is widely regarded as one of the most notorious and successful drug traffickers in history.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a poor household with his mother, Hermilda, and his father, Abel. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty, and he was forced to drop out of school at a young age to work and support his family.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar\'s involvement in organized crime began in the 1970s, when he started smuggling marijuana and cocaine into the United States. He quickly rose through the ranks of the Medellín cartel, which was founded by his mentor, Carlos Lehder. In 1979, Escobar became the leader of the cartel after Lehder\'s arrest.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel became one of the most powerful and feared organized crime groups in the world. The cartel controlled a significant portion of the global cocaine trade, and Escobar\'s wealth and influence expanded rapidly. He was known for his ruthless tactics, including murder, intimidation, and bribery, which allowed him to maintain control over his empire.\n\n**Notorious Crimes**\n\nEscobar was responsible for numerous notorious crimes, including:\n\n1. **The Palace of Justice siege**: In 1985, Escobar\'s henchmen stormed the Palace of Justice in Bogotá, Colombia, and took hundreds of people hostage. The siege ended with the deaths of over 100 people, including several Supreme Court justices.\n2. **The assassination of Luis Carlos Galán**: In 1989, Escobar ordered the assassination of presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán, who was a vocal opponent of the Medellín cartel.\n3. **The bombing of Avianca Flight 203**: In 1989, Escobar\'s henchmen planted a bomb on an Avianca flight, which exploded mid-air, killing 110 people.\n\n**Manhunt and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a luxurious prison he had built for himself. However, he escaped in 1992 and went into hiding. A massive manhunt ensued, and on December 1, 1993, Escobar was shot and killed by Colombian police in a rooftop shootout in Medellín.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He was a ruthless and cunning crime boss who was responsible for thousands of deaths and immense suffering. However, he was also a charismatic figure who was seen as a folk hero by some in Colombia, particularly in the poor neighborhoods where he grew up. His story has been immortalized in numerous films, books, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n1. **Wealth**: At the height of his power, Escobar\'s net worth was estimated to be over $30 billion, making him one of the richest people in the world.\n2. **Hacienda Nápoles**: Escobar\'s luxurious estate, Hacienda Nápoles, was a sprawling complex that included a private zoo, a lake, and a collection of exotic cars.\n3. **Family**: Escobar was married to María Victoria Henao, and they had two children together, Juan Pablo and Manuela.\n4. **Extradition**: Escobar was opposed to extradition, and he famously declared \"Me prefiero una tumba en Colombia a una cárcel en los Estados Unidos\" (\"I prefer a grave in Colombia to a prison in the United States\").', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-28 10:45:00', '2026-04-28 10:45:26', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-04-28 10:45:26', '2026-04-28 10:45:26'),
(2527, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord, crime boss, and founder of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and feared organized crime groups in history. He is widely regarded as one of the most notorious and successful drug traffickers of all time.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nEscobar was born in Rionegro, Colombia, to a middle-class family. He grew up in a poor neighborhood in Medellín, where he became involved in crime at a young age. He dropped out of school in the seventh grade and began working as a car thief and a smuggler.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar started working for the Medellín cartel, which was led by Carlos Lehder. He quickly rose through the ranks and became a key player in the organization. In 1979, Escobar and his partners assassinated Lehder and took control of the cartel.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel became one of the most powerful and feared organized crime groups in the world. The cartel controlled much of the cocaine trade in Colombia and was responsible for smuggling tons of the drug into the United States and other countries.\n\nEscobar\'s success was due in part to his innovative and ruthless business tactics. He invested heavily in infrastructure, including roads, warehouses, and communication systems. He also used violence and intimidation to eliminate competitors and maintain control over the cartel\'s operations.\n\n**Personal Life**\n\nEscobar was known for his flamboyant and extravagant lifestyle. He owned several luxurious homes, including a sprawling estate called Hacienda Napoles, which featured a private zoo, a lake, and a collection of exotic cars. He was also a fan of soccer and owned a team called Atlético Nacional.\n\n**Downfall**\n\nIn the late 1980s and early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a series of attacks on the Medellín cartel. Escobar\'s organization was weakened by internal conflicts and external pressure, and he was eventually forced to go into hiding.\n\nOn December 1, 1993, Escobar was killed in a shootout with Colombian police in Medellín. He was 44 years old at the time of his death.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning crime lord who was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people. However, he is also revered by some as a folk hero and a symbol of Colombian pride.\n\nIn recent years, Escobar\'s life and legacy have been the subject of numerous books, films, and television shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\" His story continues to fascinate and horrify people around the world, and his impact on the global drug trade and organized crime is still felt today.\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known as \"El Patrón\" (The Boss) or \"Don Pablo\" by his associates and admirers.\n* He was estimated to be worth over $30 billion at the height of his power, making him one of the richest people in the world.\n* Escobar was a fan of the Colombian soccer team and was known to have bribed referees and players to influence the outcome of games.\n* He was also a patron of the arts and was known to have supported various Colombian artists and musicians.\n* Escobar\'s death was seen as a major victory for the Colombian government and the war on drugs, but it also led to a power vacuum that was filled by other cartels and organized crime groups.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-28 10:50:00', '2026-04-28 10:50:20', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-04-28 10:50:20', '2026-04-28 10:50:20'),
(2528, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:53', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord and leader of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and notorious cocaine trafficking organizations in history. He is widely regarded as one of the most infamous and successful criminals of the 20th century.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia, to Abel de Jesús Escobar and Hermilda Gaviria. He grew up in a middle-class family and was the third of seven children. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and violence, which would later shape his involvement in organized crime.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar\'s involvement in organized crime began in the 1970s, when he worked as a small-time smuggler and thief. He quickly rose through the ranks of the Medellín cartel, which was founded by Carlos Lehder and later led by Escobar. The cartel\'s success was largely due to Escobar\'s innovative and ruthless tactics, which included bribing officials, intimidating rivals, and eliminating anyone who stood in his way.\n\n**The Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel became one of the most powerful and profitable cocaine trafficking organizations in the world. The cartel\'s operations spanned the globe, with connections in Colombia, the United States, Mexico, and other countries. Escobar\'s organization was responsible for smuggling hundreds of tons of cocaine into the United States, earning him an estimated $30 billion in profits.\n\n**Violence and Intimidation**\n\nEscobar\'s rise to power was marked by brutal violence and intimidation. He was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including rival gang members, police officers, judges, and politicians. His organization was known for its use of car bombs, assassinations, and other forms of terrorism to eliminate opponents and maintain control.\n\n**The Hunt for Escobar**\n\nIn the late 1980s and early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a massive manhunt for Escobar. The hunt was led by the Colombian National Police, who were aided by the US DEA and other international agencies. Escobar was eventually killed on December 1, 1993, in a shootout with Colombian police in Medellín.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning criminal who brought violence and destruction to Colombia and beyond. However, he is also remembered as a folk hero and a symbol of resistance against the Colombian government and the United States. His life and death have been the subject of numerous books, films, and documentaries, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a massive hacienda in Medellín.\n* He was a skilled politician and was elected to the Colombian Congress in 1982.\n* Escobar was responsible for the construction of many public works projects in Medellín, including roads, schools, and hospitals.\n* He was a devout Catholic and was known to have made large donations to the Catholic Church.\n* Escobar\'s death was seen as a major victory for the Colombian government and the international community, but it also led to a power vacuum that was filled by other drug cartels and organized crime groups.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-28 10:53:00', '2026-04-28 10:53:18', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-04-28 10:53:18', '2026-04-28 10:53:18'),
(2529, 'Auto Post for facebook at 11:18', '**Embracing the Fire Within: Lessons from a Dragon\'s Den**\n\nAs a dragon, I\'ve learned a thing or two about perseverance, passion, and leadership. Here are some key takeaways:\n\n* **Set your goals ablaze**: Don\'t be afraid to dream big and chase your aspirations with fierce determination.\n* **Hoarding knowledge is key**: Continuously learn and grow, just like a dragon\'s treasure hoard, to stay ahead of the curve.\n* **Breathing fire is not just about aggression**: It\'s about being bold, taking risks, and standing up for what you believe in.\n* **Scale new heights**: Don\'t be limited by your current surroundings - strive to reach new heights and explore new opportunities.\n* **Protect your treasure**: Prioritize self-care and safeguard your mental and physical well-being, just like a dragon guards its treasure.\n\n#Trending #DragonEnergy #MotivationMonday #LeadershipLessons #PersonalGrowth #GoalSetting #KnowledgeIsPower #BeBold #SelfCare #MentalHealthMatters #WellnessWednesday #FacebookInspiration #DragonSquad #FireWithin', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-28 11:18:00', '2026-04-28 11:18:15', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 42, '2026-04-28 11:18:15', '2026-04-28 11:18:15');
