(2566, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:35', 'As a dragon in the business world, I\'d like to share some relatable points about being a mythical creature in a modern industry. Here\'s my post for Facebook:\n\n\"Being a dragon in business can be challenging, but it also has its perks. Here are a few things I\'ve learned along the way:\n\n* My fiery personality can be an asset in negotiations, but it\'s also important to know when to hold back.\n* Having scales can be a real advantage when it comes to withstanding criticism or negative feedback.\n* My ability to fly gives me a unique perspective on the world and allows me to approach problems from a different angle.\n* Hoarding treasure can be a useful skill when it comes to managing finances and investing in the right opportunities.\n\n#DragonLife #BusinessTips #MythicalCreaturesInBusiness #TrendingNow #FireBreathingDeals #ScaleNewHeights #FlyingHighInBusiness #TreasuredInvestments\"\n\nThis post aims to showcase the professional voice of the dragon brand while also highlighting the unique challenges and advantages of being a mythical creature in the business world. The hashtags used are a mix of trending topics and industry-specific keywords to increase visibility and engagement.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-29 10:35:00', '2026-04-29 10:35:17', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 50, '2026-04-29 10:35:17', '2026-04-29 10:35:17'),
(2567, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord, smuggler, and leader of the Medellín cartel. He is widely regarded as one of the most infamous and successful drug traffickers in history.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia, to a poor farming family. He grew up in a humble household with his parents and several siblings. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and a lack of opportunities, which may have contributed to his involvement in crime.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar\'s involvement in organized crime began in the 1970s, when he started smuggling cocaine and other illicit substances. He quickly rose through the ranks of the Medellín cartel, which was founded by Carlos Lehder and other Colombian traffickers. Escobar\'s cunning, intelligence, and ruthlessness helped him become a key player in the cartel, and he eventually took over as its leader.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel became one of the most powerful and feared drug trafficking organizations in the world. The cartel\'s operations spanned across Colombia, the United States, and other countries, with Escobar\'s network of smugglers, distributors, and money launderers generating billions of dollars in revenue.\n\n**Violence and Intimidation**\n\nEscobar was known for his brutal tactics, using violence and intimidation to maintain control over his empire and eliminate his enemies. He was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including rival gang members, law enforcement officials, politicians, and innocent civilians. Escobar\'s henchmen, known as \"sicarios,\" carried out many of these killings, often using brutal methods such as torture and mutilation.\n\n**Pursuit and Death**\n\nIn the late 1980s and early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a series of operations to capture Escobar and dismantle the Medellín cartel. Escobar went into hiding, but he was eventually tracked down and killed in a shootout with Colombian police on December 1, 1993, in Medellín. He was 44 years old at the time of his death.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning crime lord who brought terror and violence to Colombia and other countries. However, he is also seen by some as a folk hero, who used his wealth and power to benefit his community and provide for his family. Escobar\'s life and death have been the subject of numerous books, films, and documentaries, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included owning a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a sprawling estate called Hacienda Napoles.\n* He was a skilled soccer player and even sponsored a local soccer team in Medellín.\n* Escobar was a devout Catholic and had a strong sense of family loyalty, which motivated many of his actions.\n* He was a skilled manipulator and used his charm and charisma to influence others and achieve his goals.\n\nOverall, Pablo Escobar\'s life and legacy are a fascinating and disturbing example of the power and influence of organized crime, and the devastating consequences of the drug trade.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-29 10:45:00', '2026-04-29 10:45:18', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-04-29 10:45:18', '2026-04-29 10:45:18'),
(2568, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord, smuggler, and leader of the Medellín Cartel. He is widely considered one of the most infamous and powerful criminals of the 20th century.\n\n**Early Life and Rise to Power**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a poor family and was raised by his mother, Hermilda, after his father abandoned the family. Escobar dropped out of school in the seventh grade and began his life of crime by selling stolen cars and smuggling goods.\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar became involved in the cocaine trade, which was rapidly expanding in Colombia. He quickly rose to prominence as a key player in the Medellín Cartel, a powerful and ruthless organization that dominated the global cocaine market. Escobar\'s success was due in part to his cunning, strategic thinking, and ability to build a vast network of loyal associates and distributors.\n\n**The Medellín Cartel and Cocaine Empire**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín Cartel became one of the most powerful and feared organizations in the world. At its peak, the cartel controlled an estimated 80% of the global cocaine market, with revenues exceeding $20 billion per year. Escobar\'s empire was built on a complex network of smuggling routes, money laundering operations, and bribery of government officials.\n\nEscobar\'s cocaine empire was notorious for its brutality and violence. He was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including rival gang members, police officers, judges, and politicians. His organization was also known for its use of terror tactics, including bombings, kidnappings, and assassinations.\n\n**Notorious Crimes and Feuds**\n\nSome of Escobar\'s most notorious crimes include:\n\n1. **The assassination of Luis Carlos Galán**: In 1989, Escobar ordered the assassination of Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán, who had vowed to crack down on the Medellín Cartel.\n2. **The bombing of Avianca Flight 203**: In 1989, Escobar\'s organization planted a bomb on an Avianca airliner, killing 110 people, in an attempt to intimidate the Colombian government.\n3. **The war with the Cali Cartel**: Escobar\'s feud with the Cali Cartel, led by the Rodríguez Orejuela brothers, resulted in a series of bloody battles and assassinations.\n\n**Downfall and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned in a specially built prison, known as La Catedral, which he had designed himself. However, he continued to run his empire from behind bars, and in 1992, he escaped from prison.\n\nA massive manhunt was launched, and on December 1, 1993, Escobar was killed in a shootout with Colombian police in Medellín. His death marked the end of an era in the global cocaine trade, and the Medellín Cartel was eventually dismantled.\n\n**Legacy and Cultural Impact**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning leader who built a vast and powerful empire through violence and intimidation. However, he is also seen as a symbol of the destructive power of the cocaine trade and the corrupting influence of wealth and power.\n\nEscobar\'s life and exploits have been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\" His story has captivated audiences around the world, and he remains one of the most fascinating and infamous figures in modern history.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-29 10:50:00', '2026-04-29 10:50:18', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-04-29 10:50:18', '2026-04-29 10:50:18'),
(2569, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:53', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord and the founder of the Medellín Cartel, one of the most powerful and feared criminal organizations in the world. He is considered one of the most notorious and influential figures in the history of organized crime.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a poor family and was raised by his mother, Hermilda, after his father abandoned them. Escobar was a bright and ambitious student, but he dropped out of school at a young age to focus on his entrepreneurial ventures.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar\'s entry into the world of organized crime began in the 1970s, when he started smuggling marijuana and cocaine into the United States. He quickly built a reputation as a ruthless and cunning businessman, and his network of smugglers and distributors expanded rapidly.\n\nIn the early 1980s, Escobar formed the Medellín Cartel, which would become one of the most powerful and feared cartels in the world. He used his wealth and influence to bribe officials, intimidate rivals, and eliminate anyone who stood in his way.\n\n**Notorious Crimes**\n\nEscobar was responsible for numerous notorious crimes, including:\n\n1. **The Assassination of Justice Minister Rodrigo Lara Bonilla**: In 1984, Escobar ordered the assassination of Justice Minister Rodrigo Lara Bonilla, who had been cracking down on the Medellín Cartel.\n2. **The Bombing of Avianca Flight 203**: In 1989, Escobar ordered the bombing of Avianca Flight 203, which killed 110 people.\n3. **The Murder of Presidential Candidate Luis Carlos Galán**: In 1989, Escobar ordered the murder of presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán, who had vowed to crack down on the Medellín Cartel.\n\n**Manhunt and Death**\n\nIn 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was sentenced to prison. However, he escaped from prison in 1992 and went into hiding. The Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a massive manhunt for Escobar.\n\nOn December 1, 1993, Escobar was shot and killed by Colombian police in a rooftop shootout in Medellín. His death marked the end of the Medellín Cartel\'s reign of terror.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning businessman who built a vast criminal empire, but also as a folk hero who provided jobs and economic opportunities for many people in Colombia.\n\nThe Netflix series \"Narcos\" (2015-2017) tells the story of Escobar\'s rise and fall, and has helped to revive interest in his life and legacy.\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n1. **Wealth**: At the height of his power, Escobar\'s net worth was estimated to be around $30 billion.\n2. **Hacienda Nápoles**: Escobar\'s estate, Hacienda Nápoles, was a sprawling complex that included a zoo, a lake, and a private airport.\n3. **Family**: Escobar was married to María Victoria Henao, and they had two children together.\n4. **Philanthropy**: Escobar was known for his philanthropic efforts, particularly in his hometown of Medellín, where he built roads, schools, and hospitals.\n\nOverall, Pablo Escobar\'s life and legacy are a fascinating and complex topic, and his impact on the world of organized crime and Colombian history continues to be felt today.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-04-29 10:53:00', '2026-04-29 10:53:20', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-04-29 10:53:20', '2026-04-29 10:53:20'),
