(3295, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:35', 'As a dragon, I understand the importance of staying connected with my audience on Facebook. Here\'s a relatable caption in points that highlights my business as a creator:\n\n**Embracing My Inner Fire**\n\nAs a dragon, I\'ve learned to harness my passions and turn them into creative pursuits. Here are a few things that drive me:\n* **Innovative thinking**: I\'m always looking for new ways to solve problems and create engaging content.\n* **Community building**: I believe in fostering a sense of belonging among my followers and encouraging them to support one another.\n* **Continuous learning**: I\'m committed to staying up-to-date with the latest trends and best practices in my industry.\n\nBy following these principles, I aim to inspire and motivate my audience to pursue their own creative endeavors. #DragonCreator #FacebookForBusiness #TrendingNow #InnovationNation #CommunityFirst #LearnAndGrow #ProfessionalDevelopment #SocialMediaMarketing #ContentCreation #InspirationMode #MotivationMonday #CreativityUnleashed #DragonFire', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-05-20 10:35:00', '2026-05-20 10:35:23', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 50, '2026-05-20 10:35:23', '2026-05-20 10:35:23'),
(3296, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord and the founder of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and notorious crime organizations in history. Here are some key facts about his life and reign:\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a poor household with his mother, Hermilda, and his father, Abel. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and violence, which would later shape his worldview and motivations.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar\'s involvement in crime began in the 1970s, when he started selling cocaine and other illicit substances in Medellín. He quickly rose through the ranks of the Colombian underworld, establishing himself as a ruthless and cunning leader. In the early 1980s, Escobar founded the Medellín cartel, which would become one of the most powerful crime organizations in the world.\n\n**The Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel dominated the global cocaine trade, smuggling massive quantities of the substance into the United States and other countries. The cartel\'s operations were characterized by violence, intimidation, and corruption, with Escobar using his wealth and influence to bribe officials and eliminate rivals.\n\n**Violence and Notoriety**\n\nEscobar\'s reign was marked by extreme violence, including assassinations, bombings, and massacres. He was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including police officers, judges, politicians, and civilians. His notoriety was further fueled by his extravagant lifestyle, which included a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a sprawling estate known as Hacienda Napoles.\n\n**Pursuit and Death**\n\nIn the late 1980s and early 1990s, Escobar\'s empire began to crumble under the pressure of law enforcement efforts and internal power struggles. In 1991, he surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned, but he escaped in 1992. A massive manhunt ensued, with the Colombian police and the US DEA working together to track down Escobar. On December 1, 1993, Escobar was killed in a shootout with Colombian police in Medellín, just one day after his 44th birthday.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning crime lord, responsible for unimaginable violence and suffering. However, he is also seen by some as a folk hero, who rose from poverty to become one of the most powerful men in Colombia. His life and reign have been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar\'s personal wealth was estimated to be around $30 billion at the height of his power.\n* He was known for his generosity, building schools, hospitals, and other infrastructure projects in Medellín.\n* Escobar\'s favorite car was a Mercedes-Benz 600, which he had armored and equipped with a phone and a TV.\n* He was a fan of soccer and owned a team called Atlético Nacional, which won several championships during his ownership.\n* Escobar\'s brother, Roberto, was also involved in the Medellín cartel and was arrested in 1990.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-05-20 10:45:00', '2026-05-20 10:45:29', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-05-20 10:45:29', '2026-05-20 10:45:29'),
(3297, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord, crime boss, and founder of the Medellín Cartel. He is widely regarded as one of the most notorious and successful criminals in history.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia. He grew up in a poor household with his mother, Hermilda, and his father, Abel. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty and violence, which would later shape his worldview and inform his actions.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar\'s entry into the world of organized crime began in the 1970s, when he started smuggling contraband goods, including cigarettes and electronics, into Colombia. He quickly expanded his operations to include cocaine, which was in high demand in the United States.\n\nIn the early 1980s, Escobar formed the Medellín Cartel, a powerful and ruthless organization that dominated the global cocaine trade. The cartel\'s success was fueled by Escobar\'s cunning, brutality, and strategic thinking. He established a network of smuggling routes, bribed officials, and used violence to eliminate competitors and maintain control.\n\n**Notorious Crimes and Feats**\n\nEscobar\'s crimes and exploits are the stuff of legend:\n\n1. **The Palace of Hacienda Napoles**: Escobar built a lavish estate, complete with a private zoo, a lake, and a collection of exotic animals.\n2. **The Assassination of Luis Carlos Galán**: Escobar ordered the murder of presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán in 1989, which led to a nationwide outcry and a crackdown on the Medellín Cartel.\n3. **The Bombing of Avianca Flight 203**: Escobar\'s henchmen planted a bomb on a commercial airliner, killing 110 people, in an attempt to assassinate a rival cartel leader.\n4. **The Escape from La Catedral**: Escobar escaped from a prison he had built for himself, La Catedral, in 1992, using a network of tunnels and loyal accomplices.\n\n**Downfall and Death**\n\nThe Colombian government, aided by the United States, launched a massive manhunt for Escobar in the early 1990s. On December 1, 1993, Escobar\'s 44th birthday, he was cornered by Colombian police in a rooftop shootout in Medellín. He was shot and killed, marking the end of an era in the world of organized crime.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted:\n\n1. **The Narco-Trafficante**: Escobar\'s success in the cocaine trade helped establish Colombia as a major player in the global narcotics industry.\n2. **The Robin Hood Effect**: Escobar\'s charitable donations and populist rhetoric earned him a degree of popularity among some Colombians, who saw him as a folk hero.\n3. **The Cartel\'s Impact on Society**: The Medellín Cartel\'s violence, corruption, and economic disruption had a profound impact on Colombian society, contributing to a decades-long cycle of violence and instability.\n\nIn recent years, Escobar\'s life and legacy have been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-05-20 10:50:00', '2026-05-20 10:50:20', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-05-20 10:50:20', '2026-05-20 10:50:20'),
(3298, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:53', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord, crime boss, and founder of the Medellín Cartel. He is widely regarded as one of the most notorious and successful criminals in history.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia, to Abel de Jesús Dari Escobar and Hermilda Gaviria. He grew up in a poor household with his parents and siblings. Escobar\'s early life was marked by poverty, and he turned to crime at a young age.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar\'s rise to power began in the 1970s, when he started smuggling marijuana and cocaine into the United States. He quickly built a network of smugglers, distributors, and money launderers, and his operation expanded rapidly. In the early 1980s, Escobar formed the Medellín Cartel, which would become one of the most powerful and feared drug cartels in the world.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nThe Medellín Cartel was a vast and complex organization that controlled much of the global cocaine trade. Escobar was the cartel\'s leader, and he used a combination of violence, intimidation, and bribery to maintain control and expand his operation. The cartel\'s profits were enormous, and Escobar became one of the richest men in the world.\n\n**Violence and Terror**\n\nEscobar\'s reign was marked by brutal violence and terror. He ordered the killings of thousands of people, including politicians, police officers, judges, and rival gang members. He also orchestrated bombings, kidnappings, and other terrorist attacks. Escobar\'s most notorious act of violence was the assassination of Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán in 1989.\n\n**Pursuit and Death**\n\nIn the late 1980s and early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a crackdown on the Medellín Cartel. Escobar went into hiding, but he was eventually tracked down by Colombian police and U.S. DEA agents. On December 1, 1993, Escobar was shot and killed in a shootout with police in Medellín, Colombia.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning crime lord who brought terror and violence to Colombia and beyond. However, he is also remembered as a charismatic and calculating leader who built a vast and powerful organization. Escobar\'s story has been the subject of numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known as \"El Patrón\" (The Boss) or \"El Señor\" (The Lord) by his associates.\n* He was a prolific builder and investor, and he constructed many homes, hotels, and other buildings in Colombia.\n* Escobar was a fan of soccer, and he owned a team called Atlético Nacional.\n* He was known for his love of luxury cars, and he owned a collection of high-end vehicles, including a Mercedes-Benz 600 and a Porsche 911.\n* Escobar\'s personal wealth was estimated to be around $30 billion at the height of his power.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-05-20 10:53:00', '2026-05-20 10:53:20', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-05-20 10:53:20', '2026-05-20 10:53:20'),
