(3364, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:35', '**Embracing the Fire Within: Key Takeaways for Success**\n\nAs a dragon, I\'ve learned a thing or two about perseverance, passion, and drive. Here are some relatable points to consider:\n\n* Set clear goals and priorities to stay focused on what matters most\n* Surround yourself with a supportive community that encourages growth and learning\n* Don\'t be afraid to take risks and try new things - it\'s often the best way to learn and improve\n* Stay adaptable and resilient in the face of challenges and setbacks\n* Keep a positive mindset and attitude, even when faced with obstacles\n\nBy following these principles, you can unlock your full potential and achieve greatness. Remember to stay true to yourself and your values, and always keep moving forward.\n\n#MotivationMonday #SuccessTips #DragonInspiration #TrendingNow #FacebookMotivation #BusinessTips #PersonalGrowth #ProfessionalDevelopment #InspirationalQuotes #RelatableContent', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-05-22 10:35:00', '2026-05-22 10:35:23', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/A_highly_detailed_fantasy_dragon_flying_over_a_medieval_castle_during_sunset_with_cinematic_lighting_and_dramatic_clouds?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 50, '2026-05-22 10:35:23', '2026-05-22 10:35:23'),
(3365, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:45', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a Colombian drug lord and the founder of the Medellín cartel, one of the most powerful and notorious drug trafficking organizations in history. He is widely regarded as one of the most successful and infamous criminals of the 20th century.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia, to a family of modest means. His father was a farmer, and his mother was a schoolteacher. Escobar grew up in a poor neighborhood in Medellín, where he became involved in street crime and smuggling at a young age.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nIn the 1970s, Escobar began working for a local cocaine smuggler, and he quickly rose through the ranks. He became known for his cunning, ruthlessness, and ability to evade law enforcement. In 1976, he founded the Medellín cartel, which would become one of the largest and most powerful drug trafficking organizations in the world.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nUnder Escobar\'s leadership, the Medellín cartel dominated the global cocaine trade, smuggling tons of cocaine into the United States and other countries. The cartel\'s success was fueled by Escobar\'s innovative tactics, including the use of small planes and submarines to transport cocaine, and his ability to bribe and intimidate law enforcement officials.\n\n**Violence and Intimidation**\n\nEscobar was notorious for his violence and intimidation tactics. He was responsible for the murder of thousands of people, including police officers, judges, politicians, and rival gang members. He also used bombings, kidnappings, and other forms of terrorism to maintain control and eliminate his enemies.\n\n**Pursuit and Death**\n\nIn the late 1980s and early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a series of operations to capture or kill Escobar. In 1991, Escobar surrendered to Colombian authorities and was imprisoned, but he escaped in 1992. He was eventually tracked down and killed in a shootout with Colombian police on December 1, 1993, in Medellín.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning criminal who was responsible for thousands of deaths and untold suffering. However, he is also seen as a folk hero by some in Colombia, who view him as a symbol of resistance against the government and the wealthy elite. His story has been immortalized in numerous books, films, and TV shows, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\"\n\n**Interesting Facts**\n\n* Escobar was known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included owning a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a mansion with a private airstrip.\n* He was a prolific philanthropist, and his charitable donations earned him the nickname \"Robin Hood\" among some Colombians.\n* Escobar\'s net worth was estimated to be around $30 billion at the height of his power, making him one of the richest people in the world.\n* He was a skilled politician and was able to manipulate the Colombian government and media to his advantage.\n* Escobar\'s death was seen as a major victory for the Colombian government and the international community, but it did not mark the end of the drug trade in Colombia.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-05-22 10:45:00', '2026-05-22 10:45:33', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-05-22 10:45:33', '2026-05-22 10:45:33'),
(3366, 'Auto Post for facebook at 10:50', 'Pablo Escobar (1949-1993) was a notorious Colombian drug lord, smuggler, and leader of the Medellín cartel. He is widely regarded as one of the most infamous and successful drug traffickers in history.\n\n**Early Life**\n\nPablo Emilio Escobar Gaviria was born on December 1, 1949, in Rionegro, Colombia, to a poor farming family. He grew up in a humble household with his mother, Hermilda, and his father, Abel. Escobar was the third of seven children. He dropped out of school in the seventh grade and began working on the streets, selling fruit and other goods to support his family.\n\n**Rise to Power**\n\nEscobar\'s entry into the world of organized crime began in the 1970s, when he started smuggling cigarettes and other contraband. He quickly moved on to cocaine, which became the primary source of his wealth and power. In the early 1980s, Escobar formed the Medellín cartel, a powerful and ruthless organization that dominated the global cocaine trade.\n\n**Medellín Cartel**\n\nThe Medellín cartel, also known as the \"Organization,\" was a vast network of smugglers, distributors, and money launderers. Escobar was the mastermind behind the operation, using his charisma, intelligence, and brutality to maintain control. The cartel\'s success was built on Escobar\'s innovative tactics, including the use of small planes to transport cocaine from Colombia to the United States.\n\n**Violence and Intimidation**\n\nEscobar was known for his ruthless tactics, using violence and intimidation to eliminate competitors, law enforcement, and anyone who stood in his way. He was responsible for the deaths of thousands of people, including politicians, judges, and police officers. His most notorious act was the assassination of Colombian presidential candidate Luis Carlos Galán in 1989.\n\n**Personal Life**\n\nDespite his notorious reputation, Escobar was a devoted family man. He was married to María Victoria Henao, and they had two children, Juan Pablo and Manuela. Escobar was also known for his extravagant lifestyle, which included owning a private zoo, a collection of luxury cars, and a lavish mansion, Hacienda Napoles.\n\n**Downfall and Death**\n\nIn the early 1990s, the Colombian government, with the help of the United States, launched a crackdown on the Medellín cartel. Escobar\'s empire began to crumble, and he was forced to go into hiding. On December 1, 1993, Escobar was shot and killed by Colombian police in a rooftop shootout in Medellín.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nPablo Escobar\'s legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a ruthless and cunning crime lord, but also as a folk hero and a symbol of resistance against the Colombian government. His life has been the subject of numerous books, films, and documentaries, including the popular Netflix series \"Narcos.\" Despite his notorious reputation, Escobar remains a fascinating and intriguing figure, whose impact on the world of organized crime continues to be felt today.', '[\"facebook\"]', 'published', 1, NULL, '2026-05-22 10:50:00', '2026-05-22 10:50:25', 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'https://image.pollinations.ai/prompt/pablo_escobar?width=1024&height=1024&nologo=true', NULL, 'pending', 52, '2026-05-22 10:50:25', '2026-05-22 10:50:25');
